tel 1.0

base telekinesis
import soundness

info   HTTP as typed values: requests, responses, headers, bodies, cookies, and a client whose transport is chosen by import
detail
    _Telekinesis_ is Soundness's model of HTTP and its client. A request is sent to a URL
    checked as the code compiles; headers are named arguments whose values are checked
    against the header they set (`accept` takes a `MediaType`, `authorization` an `Auth`);
    a request body is a typed value which knows its own media type (`Postable`); and the
    response body is read as whatever type is asked for (`Receivable`). A failing status is
    an `Http.Error` and a connection which cannot be made is a `Connect.Error`, each a typed
    error raised through a `Tactic` rather than an exception to guess at. That a piece of
    code reaches the network is itself visible: every request demands an `Online`
    capability, imported as `internetAccess.online`.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
    import internetAccess.online
    import logging.silentLogging
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import charsets.utf8Charset
    import codepages.utf8Codepage

    val page: Text = url"https://example.com/".fetch(accept = media"text/html").receive[Text]
    val status: Http.Status = url"https://example.com/".fetch().status
    val reply = url"https://example.com/submit".submit(Http.Post)(t"Hello world")
    ```

    The same vocabulary serves the other direction. `Http.Request` is what a server parses
    off a connection (`Http.Request.parse`), `Http.Response` is what a handler returns
    (`Http.Response(Http.Ok)(t"hello")`, or any `Servable` value), `Cookie`, `Parameter` and
    `Submission` read a request's cookies and form fields, and `Http.Connection` is one live
    exchange as a handler sees it. The `scintillate` library's servers are built on these
    types, and the `Servable`/`Postable` pairs the data-format libraries provide
    (`postables.jsonPostable`, `servables.xmlServable`, …) plug into both sides.

    The central types are all nested in `Http`: `Http.Request`, `Http.Response`,
    `Http.Header`, `Http.Method` (with `Http.Get`, `Http.Post`, …), `Http.Status` (with
    `Http.Ok`, `Http.NotFound`, …, exported so that `Http.Ok` is the status), `Http.Body`,
    `Http.Backend`, `Http.Client`, `Http.Session` and `Http.Connection`. A URL is urticose's
    `HttpUrl` (`url"…"`), a query string is legerdemain's `Query`, and a media type is
    gesticulate's `MediaType` (`media"…"`).

    The library is split into components by what each platform can do:

    - `telekinesis.core` — the model (`Http` and everything in it), the typeclasses
      `Postable`, `Receivable`, `Servable`, `Fetchable` and `Directive`, the `fetch` and
      `submit` extensions, `Auth`, `Cookie`, `Session`, `Context`, `Parameter`, `Submission`,
      `Connect.Error`, and the choice packages `httpRedirections`, `queryParameters` and
      `construables`. Platform-neutral; the client logic (redirects, logging) lives here and
      delegates the wire to an `Http.Backend`.
    - `telekinesis.forms` — `Orchestrate`/`orchestrate` and `Submission#form`: a form
      round-trip rendered with honeycomb's HTML and legerdemain's widgets.
    - `telekinesis.sockets` — `httpBackends.soundnessHttp`, the backend which speaks the
      HTTP/1.1 wire codec directly over coaxial's sockets (JVM and Scala Native), with
      pooled keep-alive connections; on the JVM its `https` path negotiates HTTP/2 by ALPN.
    - `telekinesis.jvm` — `httpBackends.javaNetHttp` over `java.net.http`, and the Unix
      domain-socket client (`domainSocketFetchable`, `domainSocketHttpClient`,
      `requestTransmissible`).
    - `telekinesis.http2` — the HTTP/2 layer (JVM only): `Http2` with its `Connection`,
      `Stream`, `Frame`, `Event` and `Error`, the `Hpack` and `Huffman` codecs, `FrameReader`
      and `PseudoHeaders`, the `Http.Session` implementations and the `httpUrlSessional`
      given which opens a session on a URL (`url.session: session ?=> …`). Each has its own
      page.
    - `telekinesis.wasi` — `httpBackends.wasiHttp`, a backend over `wasi:http` for a Wasm
      component, `WasiHttpServer` for serving from one, and `wasiApis.wasiHttpApi`.

    Three choices are made by importing a given by name, since `import soundness.*` does not
    import givens: the **transport** from `httpBackends` (`javaNetHttp`, `soundnessHttp`,
    `wasiHttp`), the **redirect policy** from `httpRedirections` (`doNotFollowRedirects`;
    without it, redirects are followed up to `Http.Redirection`'s limit of ten), and, for a
    generically-derived `Query`, `queryParameters.arbitraryQueryParameter`. A request also
    needs an `Http.Event is Loggable` in scope (`logging.silentLogging` discards the
    events), and a `Tactic[Connect.Error]` and `Tactic[Http.Error]` or a strategy such as
    `strategies.throwUnsafely`.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, where the choice packages merge with
    the umbrella's own.

refer telekinesis.Http
refer telekinesis.fetch
refer telekinesis.submit
refer telekinesis.httpBackends
refer telekinesis.Postable
refer telekinesis.Receivable
refer telekinesis.Servable
refer telekinesis.Connect

entry .Http   the HTTP model: methods, statuses, headers, bodies, requests, responses, clients
  detail
      `Http` is the namespace for the whole model, and a sealed trait with no instances of
      its own. The methods are `Http.Get`, `Http.Post`, `Http.Head`, `Http.Put`,
      `Http.Delete`, `Http.Connect`, `Http.Options`, `Http.Trace` and `Http.Patch`, each a
      case object of `Http.Method`; the statuses are the cases of `Http.Status`, exported
      into `Http` so that `Http.Ok`, `Http.NotFound` and the rest are reached directly. A
      message is an `Http.Request` or an `Http.Response`, each with a `List[Http.Header]`
      and an `Http.Body`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val response = Http.Response(Http.Ok, contentType = media"text/plain")(t"hello")
      response.status            // Http.Ok
      response.status.code       // 200
      response.headers.contentType
      response.body.stream.memoize.utf8
      ```

      The client side is `Http.Fetch` and `Http.Submit`, the values `url.fetch` and
      `url.submit` return and whose `apply` builds and sends the request; `Http.Client`
      runs the redirect loop and logging over an `Http.Backend`, the transport chosen from
      `httpBackends`. The server side parses with `Http.Request.parse`, answers through an
      `Http.Connection`, and serialises with `Http.Response.serialize`. `Http.Session` is
      one connection to one origin over which several requests are exchanged.

      The errors are `Http.Error` (a response outside the success range), `Http.Request.Error`
      and `Http.Response.Error` (a message which could not be parsed), and `Connect.Error` (no
      response at all).
  refer telekinesis.Http.Request
  refer telekinesis.Http.Response
  refer telekinesis.Http.Client
  refer telekinesis.Http.Backend
  refer telekinesis.Http.Status
  refer telekinesis.Connect
  entry .Version   the protocol version of a message: one of `0.9`, `1.0`, `1.1`, `2.0`, `3.0`
    detail
        `Http.Version` is the union of the literal types `0.9 | 1.0 | 1.1 | 2.0 | 3.0`, so a
        version is written as the number itself: `Http.Request(Http.Get, 1.1, …)`. The
        companion renders and parses it: `1.1` shows as `HTTP/1.1`, `2.0` as `HTTP/2`.
    entry .showable   renders a version as `HTTP/1.1`, `HTTP/2`, `HTTP/3`
    entry .parse   reads `HTTP/1.1`, `HTTP/2` or `HTTP/2.0`, `HTTP/3`; anything else is `1.0`
        Lenient by design: a version the parser does not recognise is taken as `1.0`, the
        version a server must assume for a client which sends nothing better.
  entry .Header   one header field: a key and a value, both text
    detail
        `Http.Header(key, value)`, as read off the wire or built from a `Directive`. Keys
        are case-insensitive, and the client sends them lower-case in kebab form
        (`content-type`) when they come from a named argument. A `Header` is `Encodable in
        Http.Header` (itself), so it can be added to a response with `response + header`.
    entry .encodable   the identity: a `Header` encodes as itself, so `response + header` works
    entry .repeatable   whether a field may appear more than once in a message
        `Http.Header.repeatable(t"set-cookie")` is `true`; `repeatable(t"content-type")` is
        `false`. The list-based fields (`accept`, `cache-control`, `vary`, …) and `Set-Cookie`
        (which must be repeated, since a cookie value may contain a comma) are repeatable;
        every other field, including any the library does not know, is a singleton, since
        duplicating a singleton is a protocol error. The lookup is case-insensitive. The
        rule decides how `Http.Response(…)(body)` merges a header named at the call site with
        one its `Servable` derives: a singleton is overridden, a repeatable field keeps both.
    entry #key   the field name, as written
    entry #value   the field value
  entry .Method   the request method, as a sealed trait with a case object per method
    detail
        `Http.Get`, `Http.Head`, `Http.Post`, `Http.Put`, `Http.Delete`, `Http.Connect`,
        `Http.Options`, `Http.Trace` and `Http.Patch`. Each carries, at the type level,
        whether it has a request body: `Http.Post.Payload` is `true`, `Http.Get.Payload` is
        `false`, and `#payload` is the runtime counterpart readable through the erased
        `Method` type. A method shows as its upper-case name and decodes from text
        case-insensitively, with anything unrecognised read as `GET`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val method: Http.Method = t"post".decode[Http.Method]
        method.show        // t"POST"
        method.payload     // true
        valueOf[Http.Post.Payload]
        ```

        A method is also an extractor over a request: `case Http.Post() =>` matches a
        request whose method is `Post`.
    entry .showable   renders a method as `GET`, `POST`, …
    entry .decodable   reads a method from text, case-insensitively; an unknown name is `Get`
    entry #Payload   whether the method carries a request body, as a literal `Boolean` type
    entry #payload   whether the method carries a request body, as a runtime value
        Safe to call through a value typed as the erased `Http.Method`, unlike a type-level
        `valueOf`.
    entry #unapply   `case Http.Post() =>`: matches a request whose method is this one
  entry .Get   the `GET` method; no body
  entry .Head   the `HEAD` method; no body, and a response to it has none either
  entry .Post   the `POST` method; the default of `submit`, and the one with a body
  entry .Put   the `PUT` method; has a body
  entry .Delete   the `DELETE` method; no body
  entry .Connect   the `CONNECT` method; not to be confused with `Connect.Error` or `Http.Connection`
  entry .Options   the `OPTIONS` method; no body
  entry .Trace   the `TRACE` method; no body
  entry .Patch   the `PATCH` method
  entry .Status   a response status: its code, its reason phrase, and its category
    detail
        An enum of the standard statuses, each with its `#code` and `#description`:
        `Continue` (100), `SwitchingProtocols` (101), `EarlyHints` (103), `Ok` (200),
        `Created`, `Accepted`, `NonAuthoritativeInformation`, `NoContent` (204),
        `ResetContent`, `PartialContent`, `MultipleChoices` (300), `MovedPermanently`,
        `Found` (302), `SeeOther`, `NotModified` (304), `TemporaryRedirect` (307),
        `PermanentRedirect` (308), `BadRequest` (400), `Unauthorized`, `PaymentRequired`,
        `Forbidden`, `NotFound` (404), `MethodNotAllowed`, `NotAcceptable`,
        `ProxyAuthenticationRequired`, `RequestTimeout`, `Conflict`, `Gone`,
        `LengthRequired`, `PreconditionFailed`, `PayloadTooLarge`, `UriTooLong`,
        `UnsupportedMediaType`, `RangeNotSatisfiable`, `ExpectationFailed`,
        `UnprocessableEntity` (422), `TooEarly`, `UpgradeRequired`, `PreconditionRequired`,
        `TooManyRequests` (429), `RequestHeaderFieldsTooLarge`, `UnavailableForLegalReasons`,
        `InternalServerError` (500), `NotImplemented`, `BadGateway`, `ServiceUnavailable`,
        `GatewayTimeout`, `HttpVersionNotSupported`, `VariantAlsoNegotiates`,
        `InsufficientStorage`, `LoopDetected`, `NotExtended` and
        `NetworkAuthenticationRequired` (511).

        The cases are exported into `Http`, so `Http.Ok` and `Http.Status.Ok` are the same
        value. A status is also a response constructor: `Http.Ok(headers, body)` is a
        response with that status, which is how the `Servable` givens build theirs.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Http.NotFound.code                    // 404
        Http.NotFound.description             // t"Not Found"
        Http.NotFound.category                // Http.Status.Category.ClientError
        Http.Status.unapply(503)              // Some(Http.ServiceUnavailable)
        val response = Http.Ok(Nil, Http.Body.Empty)
        ```

        A code the enum does not name has no `Status`: `Http.Status.unapply` is `None` for
        it, and a response carrying one fails to parse with `Http.Response.Error`.
    entry .unapply   the status for a code: `case Http.Status(status) =>` over an `Int`
        `Http.Status.unapply(200)` is `Some(Http.Ok)`; a code the enum does not name is
        `None`.
    entry .communicable   renders a status as a message: `404 (Not Found)`
    entry .Category   the five classes of status: informational, successful, redirection, client error, server error
        `Informational` (1xx), `Successful` (2xx), `Redirection` (3xx), `ClientError` (4xx)
        and `ServerError` (5xx). `Http.Status#category` gives a status's class, which is
        what `Receivable` checks before reading a body, and what decides the level at which
        `Http.Client` logs a response.
    entry #code   the numeric code, `200`
    entry #description   the reason phrase, `OK`
    entry #category   the status's `Http.Status.Category`, from its first digit
    entry #apply   `Http.Ok(headers, body)`: a response with this status
        A response of version `1.1` with the given headers and `Http.Body`; the result
        retains whatever the body captures.
  entry .Category   `Http.Status.Category`, exported into `Http`
      The same enum as `Http.Status.Category`, reached as `Http.Category` through the
      export of `Http.Status`'s members.
  entry .emptyBody   a fresh, already-empty request body stream
      `Http.emptyBody()` is the `Stream[Data] over Credit` with nothing in it, built anew on
      each call so that a request body `Spring` which returns it can be re-materialised
      (for a redirect, or for logging) as the `Spring` contract requires.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val empty = new Spring[Data]:
        def apply()(using Tactic[Truncation.Error]): (Stream[Data] over Credit)^ = Http.emptyBody()

      val request = Http.Request(Http.Get, 1.1, t"example.com".as[Host], t"/", Nil, empty)
      ```
  entry .Body   a message body: streamed from a spring, a fixed block of bytes, or nothing
    detail
        An enum of three cases. `Flowing(source: Spring[Data]^)` is a body produced on demand
        by a `Spring`, which can mint a fresh stream each time it is asked (a request body
        may be sent more than once, after a redirect) or, for a response read off a live
        connection, re-lend a single stream which resumes where the last reader stopped.
        `Fixed(data)` is a body whose bytes are already in hand, and `Empty` has none. A
        `Fixed` or `Empty` body is pure; a `Flowing` one retains what its spring captures.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import codepages.utf8Codepage

        val fixed = Http.Body.Fixed(t"hello".in[Data])
        fixed.stream.memoize.utf8          // t"hello"
        Http.Body.Empty.stream.memoize     // no bytes
        ```

        On the wire, `Http.Response.serialize` frames a `Fixed` or `Empty` body with
        `Content-Length` and a `Flowing` one with chunked transfer encoding.
    entry #stream   a fresh pull endpoint over the body's bytes
        Needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`, raised when a `Flowing` body is cut short. For
        `Fixed` and `Empty` the stream can be minted any number of times; for `Flowing` it
        is whatever the spring returns, which for a client response is one single-owner
        stream over the connection, so read it once, or `memoize` it.
  entry .Request   an HTTP request: method, version, host, target, headers and body
    detail
        A request as the client sends it and as the server receives it. It is built directly
        (`Http.Request(method, version, host, target, headers, body)`, the body a
        `Spring[Data]` which mints a fresh stream on each call) or parsed off a connection
        with `.parse`. The `fetch` and `submit` macros build one from a URL and its named
        header arguments.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import codepages.utf8Codepage

        val wire = t"GET /path?q=1 HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: example.com\r\nUser-Agent: test\r\n\r\n"
        val request = Http.Request.parse(Chain(wire.in[Data]))
        request.method          // Http.Get
        request.target          // t"/path?q=1"
        request.location        // t"/path"
        request.query           // Query(List(t"q" -> t"1"))
        request.host.show       // t"example.com"
        request.headers.userAgent   // List(t"test")
        ```

        The parser is lenient about the version, strict about the `Host` header (a request
        without one, or with an invalid one, raises `Http.Request.Error`), and bounded: the
        request line may not exceed 8 KiB nor the header block 64 KiB, so a hostile client
        cannot make a server read without end. A request is `Showable` as a one-line summary
        (method, host, path, headers, a sample of the body) for logging.
    refer telekinesis.Http.Response
    refer telekinesis.Http.Connection
    refer telekinesis.Parameter
    refer telekinesis.Cookie
    entry .showable   a one-line summary of the request for logs: content type, method, query, host, path, body sample, headers
    entry .serialize   the request as HTTP/1.1 wire bytes: request line, `Host`, framing, headers, body
        A fresh `Stream[Data] over Credit` of the whole message, for a transport which writes
        straight to a socket (the domain-socket client wraps this as `requestTransmissible`).
        The body is probed one block at a time: a body which ends within the first block is
        framed with `Content-Length`; anything longer streams with
        `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`. Needs a `Buffering` (for the block size) and a
        `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.
    entry .Head   the parsed request line and header block, before the body
      detail
          `Http.Request.Head(method, version, host, target, headers)`: what `.parseHead`
          returns, for a server which frames the body itself.
      entry #method   the request method
      entry #version   the protocol version
      entry #host   the `Host` header, parsed
      entry #target   the request target, path and query as written
      entry #headers   the header fields, in order
    entry .parseHead   parses the request line and headers off a cursor, leaving it at the body
        `Http.Request.parseHead(cursor, maxRequestLine = 8192, maxHeaders = 65536)` reads
        `METHOD SP target SP HTTP-version CRLF` and the header block, and stops at the first
        byte of the body without reading past it, so a `GET` with no body does not block
        waiting for bytes which will not come until the server has answered. A server driving
        one cursor across a kept-alive connection uses it with `.fixedBody` or `.chunkedBody`
        to frame the body. Raises `Http.Request.Error` with `UriTooLong` or `HeadersTooLarge`
        once the limits are crossed, `Host` for a missing or invalid `Host` header, and
        `Expectation` for a malformed line end.
    entry .parse   a request parsed off a stream of bytes
        Two forms. `parse(stream: Chain[Data])` reads from a lazy chain of blocks;
        `parse(input: Stream[Data] over Credit)` consumes a pull endpoint directly. Each
        returns the request with its body as a `Flowing` body whose spring lends the
        remainder of the input, so `request.body().memoize` reads the body. Needs a
        `Tactic[Http.Request.Error]`.
    entry .fixedBody   exactly `length` bytes of body, lent off a cursor which stays open after them
        For a request framed by `Content-Length`: the stream ends after `length` bytes and
        never reads further, leaving the cursor at the start of the next pipelined request
        on a kept-alive connection.
    entry .chunkedBody   a `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` body decoded off a cursor
        Yields each chunk's data, skipping chunk extensions and trailers, and leaves the
        cursor after the terminating `0` chunk and its blank line. Lenient: a malformed
        length or a truncated stream ends the body.
    entry .Error   raised when a request cannot be parsed
      detail
          Error 367: _could not parse HTTP request because `reason`_, raised by `.parse` and
          `.parseHead`. The reasons, numbered `367.1` to `367.5`, are tabulated under `Reason`.
      entry .Reason   why the request could not be parsed
          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `Expectation(expected, found)`  1  _`found` was found when `expected` was expected_  a malformed line end; both are `Char`s
          row  `Version(value)`  2  _the HTTP version `value` was invalid_
          row  `Host(value)`  3  _the host `value` was missing or invalid_
          row  `UriTooLong`  4  _the request line exceeded the maximum length_  `maxRequestLine`; a server answers it with `414`
          row  `HeadersTooLarge`  5  _the request headers exceeded the maximum size_  `maxHeaders`; a server answers it with `431`
          ```
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
      entry #reason   the `Http.Request.Error.Reason`
    entry #method   the method
    entry #version   the protocol version
    entry #host   the host the request is addressed to, from its `Host` header
    entry #target   the request target as written: path, and query if any
    entry #textHeaders   every header field, in order, as `Http.Header`s
    entry #body   the body as a `Spring[Data]`: call it for a fresh stream
        `request.body()` mints a stream (needing a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`); for a parsed
        request it lends the remainder of the input, so read it once or `memoize` it.
    entry #request   the request itself, for a handler's context-function receiver
    entry #path   the target's path as a `Path on Www`, rooted at `%`
        `location` decoded as a `Path on Www under %.type`, the URL path type, so segments
        can be matched with `/` patterns.
    entry #on   the request's full URL, given an origin: `request.on(origin)`
        `Url[scheme](origin, target)` for an `Origin["http"]` or `Origin["https"]`; what the
        client logs in `Http.Event.Send`.
    entry #query   the query parameters, from the URL and a form-encoded body together
        A `Query` of the target's `?…` part, plus the body decoded as
        `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` when the `Content-Type` says it is one. Reading
        the body needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`; a `Parameter` reads one field of it.
    entry #location   the target without its query: `/path` from `/path?q=1`
    entry #headers   typed access to headers by name: `request.headers.contentType`
        A dynamic selector: `request.headers.accept`, `request.headers.cookie`,
        `request.headers.contentLength` are each the `List` of that field's values, decoded
        to the type the `Directive` of that name declares (a `MediaType`, a
        `List[Cookie.Value]`, a `Bytes`), through its `Decodable in Text`. The camelCase name
        is turned into the kebab-case field name.
    entry #contentType   the `Content-Type` header as a `MediaType`, or `Unset`
    entry #textCookies   the request's cookies as a name-to-value `Map`
        Parsed from the `Cookie` header; `Cookie#apply()` reads one of them decoded to its
        type.
  entry .Backend   the transport which sends one request and returns its response
    detail
        The swappable wire. An `Http.Backend` takes the fully-resolved URL as text (so a
        non-JVM backend can parse it itself), the method, the headers and the body spring,
        and returns the response; it handles neither redirects nor logging, which
        `Http.Client` does above it. Backends are platform-specific, so one is always chosen
        by importing a given from `httpBackends` by name: `javaNetHttp` (`java.net.http`, in
        `telekinesis.jvm`), `soundnessHttp` (Soundness's own wire codec over coaxial sockets,
        in `telekinesis.sockets`, JVM and Native) or `wasiHttp` (`wasi:http`, in
        `telekinesis.wasi`).

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val backend: Http.Backend = summon[Http.Backend]
        val empty = new Spring[Data]:
          def apply()(using Tactic[Truncation.Error]): (Stream[Data] over Credit)^ = Http.emptyBody()

        val response = backend.request(t"http://127.0.0.1:8080/", Http.Get, Nil, empty)
        ```
    refer telekinesis.httpBackends
    refer telekinesis.Http.Client
    entry #request   sends one request and returns its response, raising `Connect.Error` if none comes
  entry .Response   an HTTP response: version, status, headers and body
    detail
        A response is read from a client fetch, built by a server handler, or parsed off a
        connection. Its constructor is private: a response is made with the dynamic
        `Http.Response(…)` apply, by a status (`Http.Ok(headers, body)`), or by a `Servable`
        (`Http.Response(json)` for any value with a `Servable` in scope, through
        `.conversion`).

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import codepages.utf8Codepage

        val a = Http.Response(Http.Ok)(t"hello")
        val b = Http.Response(Http.Found, location = t"/elsewhere")()
        val image: Data = t"...".in[Data]
        val c = Http.Response(contentType = media"image/jpeg")(image)
        val d = Http.Response(Http.Ok, contentType = media"text/plain")(t"hello") + Cookie.Value(t"id", t"42")
        ```

        The first argument list takes, in any order, at most one `Http.Status` and named
        headers (`contentType = media"…"`, `location = t"…"`, each checked by its
        `Directive`); the second takes the body, as a `Servable` value or an `Http.Body`. A
        header named at the call site overrides the one the `Servable` would derive for the
        same singleton field (so `contentType = …` with a `Text` body puts `content-type` on
        the wire once), while a repeatable field such as `Set-Cookie` keeps both.

        On the client, `#receive[Text]` reads the body as the type asked for, `#status`
        inspects the status, and `#headers.contentType` reads a header by name. The body is
        a `Flowing` body over the connection, read once: `#successBody` or `#body.stream`
        lends the single-owner stream. A server-side response may retain the live connection
        it answers (`Response^{connection}`); a client-facing one is always pure.
    refer telekinesis.Http.Request
    refer telekinesis.Servable
    refer telekinesis.Receivable
    entry .applyDynamicNamed   `Http.Response(Http.Ok, contentType = …)(body)`: a status and named headers, then a body
        The macro behind `Http.Response(…)`. With a single argument which has a `Servable`,
        it is that value served (`Http.Response(t"hello")`). Otherwise it collects an
        optional `Http.Status` and any number of headers — named (`contentType = …`, encoded
        by the `Directive` of that name) or unnamed (a value whose type identifies one
        header, such as a `MediaType` for `accept`) — into a `Protoresponse`, whose own
        `apply` takes the body. A status given twice is a compile error, as is a header
        value of the wrong type: _the header `Content-Type` cannot take a value of type
        `Int`_.
    entry .applyDynamic   `Http.Response(Http.Ok)(body)`: positional arguments only
        As `.applyDynamicNamed`, for a call with no named arguments.
    entry .conversion   any `Servable` value converts to a `Response`
        The `Conversion` which lets a handler return a `Json`, a `Text` or any value with a
        `Servable` in scope where an `Http.Response` is expected.
    entry .Protoresponse   a status and headers awaiting a body
      detail
          What `Http.Response(Http.Ok, contentType = …)` is before its second argument list:
          `Protoresponse(status0, headers)`, with two `apply`s to finish it.
      entry #status0   the status, or `Unset` for `Ok`
      entry #headers   the headers named at the call site
      entry #apply   finishes the response with an `Http.Body` (default `Empty`), or with a `Servable` value
          `proto()` is the response with an empty body; `proto(body: Http.Body)` attaches
          one, the result retaining what the body captures; `proto(value)` serves a value
          through its `Servable`, with the call-site headers overriding the served ones for
          singleton fields. The status, if unset, is the served response's.
    entry .streamable   a successful response streams its body; any other status raises `Http.Error`
        The `Streamable by Data over Credit` given, so a response can be `read[Data]` or
        piped on. Needs a `Tactic[Http.Error]`, raised when the status is not 2xx, and a
        `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.
    entry .serialize   the response as HTTP/1.1 wire bytes
        `Http.Response.serialize(response, includeBody = true, version = 1.1)`: the status
        line, the headers with a `Content-Length` added for a `Fixed` or `Empty` body and
        `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` framing for a `Flowing` one (unless the headers already
        frame it), the blank line and the body. `includeBody = false` is for a response to a
        `HEAD` request: the framing headers are written, the body is not. After
        `101 Switching Protocols` the body is the upgraded protocol's raw stream and no
        framing is added; to an HTTP/1.0 client a streaming body is written raw, to be
        delimited by closing the connection. Needs a `Buffering` and a
        `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        Http.Response.serialize(Http.Response(Http.NotFound)()).memoize.utf8
        // t"HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found\r\ncontent-length: 0\r\n\r\n"
        ```
    entry .serializeHead   the status line and headers alone, as one block of bytes
        The head exactly as `.serialize` would write it, framing headers included, for a
        server which sends a fixed body as a second buffer straight from the handler's bytes
        with no copy.
    entry .parse   a response parsed off a stream of bytes
        `parse(stream: Chain[Data], bodiless = false)` reads from a lazy chain;
        `parse(input: Stream[Data] over Credit)` and `parse(input, bodiless)` consume a pull
        endpoint. `bodiless` marks a response to a `HEAD` request, which repeats the `GET`
        framing headers but carries no body. The body is framed by the headers: chunked
        decoding for `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, a fixed run for `Content-Length`, nothing
        for `204`, `304`, `1xx` or a bodiless response, the whole remainder for `101`, and
        otherwise everything until the connection closes. The cursor is left at the first
        byte after the body, so a kept-alive connection can parse the next response. Needs a
        `Tactic[Http.Response.Error]`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import codepages.utf8Codepage

        val wire = t"HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nContent-Type: text/plain\r\n\r\nbody"
        val response = Http.Response.parse(Chain(wire.in[Data]))
        response.status                    // Http.Ok
        response.body.stream.memoize.utf8  // t"body"
        ```
    entry .Head   the parsed status line and header block, before the body
      detail
          `Http.Response.Head(version, status, headers)`: what `.parseHead` returns, for a
          client which frames the body itself, as an `Http.Session` does.
      entry #version   the protocol version
      entry #status   the status
      entry #headers   the header fields, in order
    entry .parseHead   parses the status line and headers off a cursor, leaving it at the body
        Reads `HTTP-version SP code SP reason CRLF` and the header block, and stops at the
        first byte of the body without reading further, so a bodiless response on a
        kept-alive connection does not block on a refill the server will never send. Raises
        `Http.Response.Error` with `Status` for a code which is not three digits beginning
        1–5, or which the `Http.Status` enum does not name, and `Expectation` for a
        malformed line.
    entry .Error   raised when a response cannot be parsed
      detail
          Error 366: _could not parse HTTP response because `reason`_, raised by `.parse` and
          `.parseHead`. The reasons are `Expectation(expected, found)` (366.1, _`found` was
          found when `expected` was expected_: the bytes did not begin `HTTP/`, or a line
          did not end in CRLF) and `Status(value)` (366.2, _the HTTP status code `value` was
          invalid_). The socket-backed clients translate it into
          `Connect.Error(Connect.Error.Reason.Unknown)`.
      entry .Reason   why the response could not be parsed: `Expectation(expected, found)` or `Status(value)`
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
      entry #reason   the `Http.Response.Error.Reason`
    entry #version   the protocol version, `1.1` for every response a client builds
    entry #status   the `Http.Status`
    entry #textHeaders   every header field, in order, as `Http.Header`s
    entry #body   the `Http.Body`
    entry #updateDynamic   `response.contentType = media"…"`: sets a header, replacing any of that name
        A dynamic setter: `response.location = t"/next"` returns a new response (the
        receiver is immutable) with the header named by the `Directive` of that name,
        encoded by it, replacing any existing field of the same name.
    entry #successBody   the body stream if the status is successful, `Unset` otherwise
        The single-owner pull endpoint over the body for a 2xx status; needs a
        `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.
    entry #receive   reads the body as a type: `response.receive[Text]`
        Summons the `Receivable` for the type and applies it. The standard ones read `Text`,
        `Http.Status`, any type `Aggregable by Data` (`Data`, a stream, a `Json`, …), and any
        type `Instantiable across HttpRequests from Text`; a non-success status raises
        `Http.Error` before anything is read.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
        import internetAccess.online
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import charsets.utf8Charset

        val text: Text = url"https://example.com/".fetch().receive[Text]
        val data: Data = url"https://example.com/".fetch().receive[Data]
        ```
    entry #headers   typed access to headers by name: `response.headers.contentType`
        A dynamic selector, as on `Http.Request`: `response.headers.setCookie` is the `List`
        of that field's values decoded to the type its `Directive` declares.
    entry #+   `response + cookie`: adds a header encoded from any value `Encodable in Http.Header`
        A `Cookie.Value` adds a `Set-Cookie` field; an `Http.Header` adds itself. The
        header is prepended; nothing is replaced (use `#updateDynamic` for that).
  entry .Submit   a request with a body, ready to be given its headers and payload
    detail
        What `url.submit` returns: the request target, host and the `Fetchable` target
        (an `Origin` for an `HttpUrl`, a `DomainSocket` for a socket endpoint). Its `apply`
        is a macro which takes the method and headers in a first argument list and the
        payload in a second, builds the `Http.Request` with a `Content-Type` from the
        payload's `Postable`, and sends it through the `Http.Client onto target` in scope.
        See `submit`.
    entry #originForm   the request target: the path and query of the URL
    entry #target   where the request goes: the URL's `Origin`, or a `DomainSocket`
    entry #host   the `Host` header's value
    entry #applyDynamicNamed   `url.submit(Http.Put, accept = media"…")(payload)`: method, named headers, then the body
        The method, if given, appears once in the first argument list and defaults to
        `Http.Post`; the rest are headers, named or unnamed (see `fetch`). The payload is
        any value with a `Postable`; the `Content-Type` is the `Postable`'s media type.
        Needs an `Online`, an `Http.Event is Loggable`, and an `Http.Client onto` the target.
    entry #applyDynamic   `url.submit(Http.Put)(payload)` or `url.submit()(payload)`: positional arguments only
  entry .Fetch   a request without a body, ready to be given its headers
    detail
        What `url.fetch` returns; its `apply` is a macro which takes the method (default
        `Http.Get`) and headers, and sends the request through the `Http.Client onto target`
        in scope. See `fetch`.
    entry #originForm   the request target: the path and query of the URL
    entry #target   where the request goes: the URL's `Origin`, or a `DomainSocket`
    entry #host   the `Host` header's value
    entry #applyDynamicNamed   `url.fetch(accept = media"…", authorization = Auth.Bearer(token))`: named headers
        Needs an `Online`, an `Http.Event is Loggable`, and an `Http.Client onto` the
        target; the body is empty.
    entry #applyDynamic   `url.fetch()` or `url.fetch(Http.Head, media"…")`: positional arguments only
  entry .Client   sends a request to a target: redirects, logging, and the `Http.Backend` beneath
    detail
        An `Http.Client onto Target` turns an `Http.Request` and a target into a response.
        For `Origin["http" | "https"]` targets, two givens in the companion compete, selected
        by whether a `Redirects.Disabled` is in scope: `.http` follows redirects and `.httpStrict`
        does not. Both need a `Tactic[Connect.Error]`, an `Online` and an `Http.Backend`; the
        request itself needs an `Http.Event is Loggable`. For a `DomainSocket` target the
        client is `domainSocketHttpClient`, in `telekinesis.jvm`.

        A client is a capability: it is built from a tactic, an `Online` token and a backend,
        all of which it retains. One is rarely summoned by hand; `fetch` and `submit` summon
        it for the target their URL gives.
    refer telekinesis.Http.Backend
    refer telekinesis.Http.Redirection
    refer telekinesis.httpRedirections
    entry .http   the redirect-following client for `http` and `https` origins
        Chosen when no `Redirects.Disabled` is in scope. Logs `Http.Event.Send` at `info`,
        sends through the `Http.Backend`, and follows `301`, `302`, `303`, `307` and `308`
        responses by their `Location` (resolved against the current URL), up to the
        `Http.Redirection` limit in scope (ten by default). A `301`/`302`/`303` downgrades
        the method to `GET` and drops the body; `307`/`308` keep both, re-minting the body
        from its spring. The discarded intermediate body is drained to free its connection,
        and each hop logs `Http.Event.Redirect` at `fine`. The final response is logged at a
        level by its category: `fail` for a 5xx, `warn` for a 4xx, `fine` otherwise. A
        redirect with no `Location`, or one past the limit, is returned as it is.
    entry .httpStrict   the client which returns a 3xx response unchanged
        Chosen when `httpRedirections.doNotFollowRedirects` (a `Redirects.Disabled`) is in
        scope. The redirect is then an ordinary response: `receive` raises `Http.Error`
        with the 3xx status, and `status` inspects it.
    entry #request   sends a request to a target and returns the response
  entry .Error   raised when a response arrives with a status outside the success range
    detail
        Error 438: _the HTTP request failed with status `status`_. Raised by `Receivable`
        (`response.receive[T]`) and by `Http.Response.streamable` when the status's category
        is not `Successful`; the error carries the status and the headers, so the caller can
        react to what went wrong. Inspecting `response.status` directly never raises it.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
        import internetAccess.online
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import charsets.utf8Charset

        capture[Http.Error](url"https://example.com/missing".fetch().receive[Text]).status  // Http.NotFound
        ```
    entry #status   the response's status
    entry #headers   the response's headers
  entry .Event   what the client logs as it sends requests and receives responses
    detail
        Every `Event` is tagged `Log.Network`, and `Send` and `Redirect` also `Log.Protocol`.
        `Http.Client` logs `Send(method, url, headers)` at `info` when a request goes out,
        `Redirect(from, to)` at `fine` for each hop it follows, and `Response(status)` at
        `fine`, `warn` (4xx) or `fail` (5xx) when the reply arrives. `Request(preview)` is a
        sample of a request body for a transport which logs it. An `Http.Event is Loggable`
        must be in scope for every `fetch` and `submit`; `logging.silentLogging` discards
        them.
    entry .communicable   renders each event as a message: _sending a GET request to …_
  entry .Redirection   the most redirects the client will follow: ten unless overridden
    detail
        `Http.Client.http` follows at most `Http.Redirection#value` hops; the companion's
        `.default` is ten (curl's limit), and a local `given Http.Redirection = Http.Redirection(1)`
        lowers it. Once the limit is reached the 3xx response is returned as it is, so a
        subsequent `receive` raises `Http.Error` with the redirect status. To follow none,
        import `httpRedirections.doNotFollowRedirects` instead.
    entry .default   the limit of ten redirects, in implicit scope
    entry #value   the number of redirects allowed
  entry .Session   one client connection to one origin, over which several requests are exchanged
    detail
        A session is opened on a URL with `url.session: session ?=> …` (the `httpUrlSessional`
        given, in `telekinesis.http2`), which connects once, lends the session to the block,
        and closes it after. The protocol is fixed for the session's lifetime: for `https`,
        ALPN during the TLS handshake chooses a multiplexed HTTP/2 session, on which fetches
        interleave over one connection, or a sequential HTTP/1.1 one; plaintext `http` is
        always sequential, with keep-alive pinning one connection across the fetches.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import soundness.httpUrlSessional
        import internetAccess.online
        import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val empty = new Spring[Data]:
          def apply()(using Tactic[Truncation.Error]): (Stream[Data] over Credit)^ = Http.emptyBody()

        val request = Http.Request(Http.Get, 1.1, t"example.com".as[Host], t"/", Nil, empty)

        url"https://example.com".session: session ?=>
          session.fetch(request).status
          session.fetch(request).status
        ```

        A session is an exclusive, stateful capability: `#fetch` is an `update` method and
        its response borrows the session, so a response body still streaming from the live
        connection cannot escape the block; a memoized one can. Leaving a response unconsumed
        does not derail the session: the next fetch drains what remains of the previous body
        to reach the response boundary. (The static rejection of an escaping handle awaits
        further separation-checker support; the types express it already.)
    refer telekinesis.Http.Client
    entry #fetch   sends a request over the session's connection and returns its response
        Needs a `Tactic[Connect.Error]`. The result retains the session.
  entry .Connection   one live request/response exchange, as a server handler sees it
    detail
        An `Http.Connection` is the `Http.Request` it carries — it extends `Request`, so a
        handler reads `connection.method`, `connection.query` and `connection.headers`
        directly — plus the means of answering it: `#respond`, a sink which writes a response
        to the client and closes the exchange, and the facts of the transport, `#tls` and
        `#port`. It is an exclusive capability, since an exchange has one owner and is
        answered once; a handler's response may retain it
        (`(connection: Http.Connection) ?=> Http.Response^{connection}`) when its body
        streams from the request. A server (scintillate's) constructs it from its transport;
        the type itself needs only the request and the sink.
    refer telekinesis.Http.Request
    entry .Respond   the sink which writes a response to the client and closes the connection
      detail
          A named single-method trait, `apply(response: Response^)(using
          Tactic[Truncation.Error]): Unit`, rather than a function type, so that the response
          may capture the live connection.
    entry #tls   whether the request arrived over TLS
    entry #port   the port the server accepted the connection on
    entry #respond   the `Http.Connection.Respond` sink for this exchange

entry .Acceptance   the outcome of validating a submitted value: accepted, or rejected with errors
    An enum with two cases: `Accept(value)` carries the value a form submission decoded to,
    and `Reject(errors)` the `Errors` which explain why it did not. It is the shape a form
    handler returns when a submission is checked before it is acted on.

entry .Auth   the credentials of an `Authorization` header, by scheme
  detail
      An enum of the authentication schemes: `Basic(username, password)`, `Bearer(token)`,
      `Digest(digest)`, `Hoba(text)`, `Mutual(text)`, `Negotiate(text)`, `OAuth(text)`,
      `ScramSha1(text)`, `ScramSha256(text)` and `Vapid(text)`. An `Auth` shows as the
      header's value (`Basic` base64-encodes `username:password`) and decodes from one, so
      it is the type the `authorization` header takes in a `fetch` and what
      `request.headers.authorization` yields.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Auth.Basic(t"user", t"pass").show         // t"Basic dXNlcjpwYXNz"
      t"Bearer abc.def".as[Auth]                // Auth.Bearer(t"abc.def")
      Auth.Basic(t"user", t"p:a:ss").show.as[Auth]
      ```

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      url"https://example.com/api".fetch(authorization = Auth.Bearer(t"token")).status
      ```
  entry .showable   renders any `Auth` case as its header value, `Bearer …`, `Basic …`
      Declared over every subtype, so a value typed as `Auth.Bearer` renders as the header
      value too, rather than falling to the generic enum rendering.
  entry .decodable   reads an `Auth` from a header value, raising `Auth.Error` for an unknown scheme
      `Basic` credentials are decoded from their one base64 text and split at the first
      colon, so a password may contain colons; a text with no colon after decoding is an
      `Auth.Error`. Needs a `Tactic[Auth.Error]`, which the instance retains.
  entry .Error   raised when a header value is not a recognised authentication scheme
    detail
        Error 570: _the authentication value `value` is not valid_, raised by `.decodable`
        for a scheme it does not know or malformed `Basic` credentials.
    entry #value   the text which could not be decoded

entry .Connect   the namespace of `Connect.Error`: a server which could not be reached
  detail
      `Connect` holds the error for failing to reach a server at all — DNS, the TCP
      connection, the TLS handshake — as distinct from `Http.Error`, which reports a response
      the server did send, and coaxial's `Socket.Error`, which reports a connection that was
      made and then failed. The neighbours are differently named on purpose: `Http.Connect`
      is the `CONNECT` method and `Http.Connection` an established exchange; neither is this.
  refer telekinesis.Http.Error
  entry .Error   raised when a request cannot reach the server: DNS, refusal, TLS, timeout
    detail
        Error 999: _the TCP connection failed because `reason`_, raised by every
        `Http.Backend` and `Http.Client`, so a `fetch` or `submit` needs a
        `Tactic[Connect.Error]` in scope. The reasons, numbered `999.1` to `999.5`, are
        tabulated under `Reason`. A TLS failure is distinguished from a network one, and its own
        `Connect.Error.Reason.Ssl.Reason` says at which stage it failed; an expired
        certificate, one for the wrong host, a revoked one or a broken cipher suite are each
        refused at the handshake rather than accepted with a warning. Where a connection must
        be made to a host whose certificate cannot be validated, a relaxed `TlsAcceptance`
        in scope permits it, explicitly.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
        import internetAccess.online
        import logging.silentLogging
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        capture[Connect.Error](url"http://no-such-host.invalid/".fetch()).reason   // Connect.Error.Reason.Dns
        capture[Connect.Error](url"https://expired.badssl.com/".fetch()).reason    // Ssl(Handshake)
        ```

        The socket-backed clients (`soundnessHttp`, the domain-socket client and sessions)
        map a `Socket.Error` onto a `Connect.Error` by reason, and report a malformed response
        (`Http.Response.Error`) as `Unknown`; `javaNetHttp` classifies the JDK's exceptions.
    entry .Reason   why the connection failed: `Dns`, `Refused`, `Ssl(reason)`, `Timeout` or `Unknown`
      detail
          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `Dns`  1  _the server address could not be resolved by DNS_
          row  `Refused`  2  _the connection was refused_
          row  `Ssl(reason)`  3  _the SSL/TLS layer could not be established because `reason`_  a `Connect.Error.Reason.Ssl.Reason`, saying where the TLS layer failed
          row  `Timeout`  4  _the server did not respond within the time limit_
          row  `Unknown`  5  _an unrecognized error occurred_
          ```
      entry .Ssl   the namespace of the TLS failure reasons
        detail
            `Connect.Error.Reason.Ssl.Reason` is the unnumbered enum of TLS failures:

            ```tabular
            column  Case
            column  Message
            column  Note
            row  `Handshake`  _the local and remote peer could not negotiate the desired level of security_  what every certificate problem and cipher-suite mismatch reports
            row  `Key`  _the SSL key was bad_
            row  `Peer`  _the remote peer's identity could not be verified_
            row  `Protocol`  _the local or remote implementation of the SSL protocol did not behave as expected_
            ```

            The socket-backed clients report every TLS failure as
            `Handshake`; `javaNetHttp` distinguishes the four from the JDK's exceptions.
        entry .Reason   `Handshake`, `Key`, `Peer` or `Protocol`
          entry .communicable   renders each TLS reason as a message
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as a message
    entry #reason   the `Connect.Error.Reason`

entry .Context   a per-session value store: one slot of a type for each `Session`
  detail
      `Context[value]()` is a store with a slot per session, read and written under a
      `Session` given: `context()` is the `Optional[value]` for the current session, and
      `context() = value` sets it. It is how server-side state (a login, a shopping basket)
      is kept across requests, keyed by the session cookie's `Session`. Backed by a
      concurrent map; a `Context` is `Findable`, so it can be summoned as a contextual
      value.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Basket(items: List[Text])
      val baskets: Context[Basket] = Context[Basket]()

      given Session = Session(t"a3f1")
      baskets() = Basket(List(t"apple"))
      baskets()                              // Basket(List(t"apple"))
      ```
  refer telekinesis.Session
  refer telekinesis.Cookie
  entry .apply   `Context[value]()`: a new, empty store
  entry #apply   the current session's value, or `Unset`
  entry #update   `context() = value`: sets the current session's value

entry .Cookie   a cookie template: a name, its attributes, and the type its value is encoded as
  detail
      A `Cookie[value]` is not a cookie which has been sent but the template one is written
      into: `Cookie[Text](t"theme")` names a cookie and its domain, expiry, `Secure`,
      `HttpOnly` and path attributes, and the type its value encodes to and decodes from
      text. `cookie(value)` makes the `Cookie.Value` to send in a `Set-Cookie` header
      (`response + cookie(value)`), and `cookie()` reads the value back from the `Cookie`
      header of the `Http.Request` in scope, decoded to its type.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val theme = new Cookie[Text](t"theme", Unset, Unset, true, true, Unset)
      val response = Http.Response(Http.Ok)(t"hello") + theme(t"dark")
      response.headers.setCookie      // List(t"theme=dark; Secure; HttpOnly")
      ```

      A `Cookie[Session]` has `Cookie.session`, which runs a block under the request's
      `Session`, minting one and setting the cookie when the request has none. Pattern
      matching on a request with `cookie.Session` extracts the decoded value. A cookie
      template inspects as `Cookie(t"theme" ╱ secure ╱ httpOnly)`, showing only the
      attributes it carries.
  refer telekinesis.Session
  refer telekinesis.Context
  entry .apply   `Cookie[value](name, domain, expiry, secure, httpOnly, path)`: a template
      The value type is given explicitly and must be `Encodable in Text` and `Decodable in
      Text`. `domain` is an `Optional[Hostname]`, `expiry` any duration type `Abstractable
      across Durations to Long` (stored in seconds), `secure` and `httpOnly` default to
      `false`, and `path` is optional text.
  entry .inspectable   renders a template as `Cookie(name ╱ domain:… ╱ expiry:… ╱ secure ╱ httpOnly)`, attributes present only
  entry .Value   a cookie as sent or received: name, value, and its attributes
    detail
        `Cookie.Value(name, value, domain, path, expiry, secure, httpOnly)` is the concrete
        cookie: what a template's `apply(value)` makes, what `request.headers.cookie`
        decodes, and what `response + cookieValue` adds as a `Set-Cookie` header. Its
        `expiry` is a `Max-Age` in seconds.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Cookie.Value(t"id", t"42", path = t"/", secure = true).show
        // t"id=42; Path=/; Secure"
        ```
    entry .showable   renders the `Set-Cookie` form: `name=value; Max-Age=…; Domain=…; Path=…; Secure; HttpOnly`
    entry .inspectable   renders as `Cookie.Value(…)` around the `Set-Cookie` form, so it is not mistaken for header text
    entry .encodable   encodes a cookie as a `Set-Cookie` header, for `response + cookie`
    entry .addable   `response + cookie`: a response with a `set-cookie` header added
    entry .decodable   reads the `Cookie` request header, `a=1; b=2`, as a `List[Cookie.Value]`
        Each `name=value` pair is URL-decoded; a fragment with no `=` is skipped.
    entry #name   the cookie's name
    entry #value   the cookie's value, as text
    entry #domain   the `Domain` attribute, if set
    entry #path   the `Path` attribute, if set
    entry #expiry   the `Max-Age` attribute in seconds, if set
    entry #secure   whether the `Secure` attribute is set
    entry #httpOnly   whether the `HttpOnly` attribute is set
  entry .session   runs a handler under the request's session, setting the session cookie if it is new
      An extension on `Cookie[Session]`: `cookie.session { … }` reads the session from the
      request in scope, or mints a new one from a UUID, runs the block with it as a given,
      and adds the cookie to the response the block returns.
  entry #name   the cookie's name
  entry #domain   the `Domain` attribute the template imposes, if any
  entry #expiry   the `Max-Age` the template imposes, in seconds, if any
  entry #secure   whether the cookie is marked `Secure`
  entry #httpOnly   whether the cookie is marked `HttpOnly`
  entry #path   the `Path` attribute the template imposes, if any
  entry #apply   `cookie(value)` makes a `Cookie.Value`; `cookie()` reads the value from the request in scope
      `cookie(value)` encodes the value to text and attaches the template's attributes.
      `cookie()` needs an `Http.Request` given, and is the `Optional[value]` decoded from
      that request's cookie of this name, `Unset` if it is absent.
  entry #Session   an extractor over the request in scope: `case cookie.Session(value) =>`
      Matches when the request has this cookie, and runs the given block with the decoded
      value as a contextual value.

entry .Directive   how a header's value type is checked and encoded, by the header's name
  detail
      A `Directive` is the typeclass behind a named header argument. `("accept" is Directive
      of MediaType)` says that the header written as `accept = …` takes a `MediaType` and
      how to render it; the macro behind `fetch`, `submit` and `Http.Response(…)` summons
      the directive for each argument's name and checks the value's type against it, so
      `accept = 3` is a compile error: _the header `Accept` cannot take a value of type
      `Int`_. The name on the wire is the camelCase label turned into kebab case:
      `contentType` becomes `content-type`. The same directives type the dynamic readers
      `request.headers.contentType` and `response.headers.setCookie`, decoding the field's
      text to the directive's type.

      An unnamed argument is matched by its type alone, so a `MediaType` on its own is an
      `accept`, and an `Auth` an `authorization`; a type which is a `Directive` for more
      than one header (`Text`) cannot be passed unnamed: _the type `Text` does not uniquely
      identify a particular HTTP header_.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      url"https://example.com/".fetch(accept = media"application/json", userAgent = t"soundness").status
      url"https://example.com/".fetch(accept = 3)   // does not compile
      ```

      The companion declares a directive for each standard header: the typed ones are
      `accept` (a `MediaType`, or a `List[MediaType]`), `authorization` (`Auth`),
      `contentType` (`MediaType`), `contentLength` (`Bytes`), `contentEncoding` (an
      `Encoding`), `cookie` (`List[Cookie.Value]`), `host` (`Host`), `maxForwards` (`Int`),
      `origin` (any URL type), `range` (an `Interval`, rendered as `bytes=start-end`) and
      `transferEncoding` (`TransferEncoding`); every other standard header takes `Text`. A
      header the companion does not name needs a local given, `given ("xRequestId" is
      Directive of Text) = identity(_)`, to be passed by name.
  refer telekinesis.fetch
  refer telekinesis.Http.Response
  entry #Self   the header's name, as a literal type: `"contentType"`
  entry #encode   renders a value of the header's type as the field's text
  entry #key   the header's name as text, from `Self`
  entry .contentEncoding   `contentEncoding`: an `Encoding`, rendered by its name
  entry .accept   `accept`: a `MediaType`
  entry .accept2   `accept`: a `List[MediaType]`, joined with commas
  entry .authorization   `authorization`: an `Auth`
  entry .cacheControl   `cacheControl`: text
  entry .connection   `connection`: text
  entry .contentMd5   `contentMd5`: text
  entry .contentType   `contentType`: a `MediaType`, rendered without its parameters
  entry .contentLength   `contentLength`: a `Bytes` quantity, rendered as a number
  entry .cookie   `cookie`: a `List[Cookie.Value]`, joined with `; `
  entry .date   `date`: text
  entry .expect   `expect`: text
  entry .forwarded   `forwarded`: text
  entry .from   `from`: text
  entry .host   `host`: a `Host`
  entry .http2Settings   `http2Settings`: text
  entry .ifMatch   `ifMatch`: text
  entry .ifModifiedSince   `ifModifiedSince`: text
  entry .ifNoneMatch   `ifNoneMatch`: text
  entry .ifRange   `ifRange`: text
  entry .ifUnmodifiedSince   `ifUnmodifiedSince`: text
  entry .maxForwards   `maxForwards`: an `Int`
  entry .origin   `origin`: any URL type `Abstractable across Urls to Text`
  entry .pragma   `pragma`: text
  entry .prefer   `prefer`: text
  entry .proxyAuthorization   `proxyAuthorization`: text
  entry .range   `range`: an `Interval` of ordinals, rendered as `bytes=start-end`
  entry .referer   `referer`: text
  entry .te   `te`: text
  entry .trailer   `trailer`: text
  entry .transferEncoding   `transferEncoding`: a `TransferEncoding`
  entry .userAgent   `userAgent`: text
  entry .upgrade   `upgrade`: text
  entry .via   `via`: text
  entry .warning   `warning`: text
  entry .acceptCharset   `acceptCharset`: text
  entry .accessControlAllowOrigin   `accessControlAllowOrigin`: text
  entry .accessControlAllowCredentials   `accessControlAllowCredentials`: text
  entry .accessControlExposeHeaders   `accessControlExposeHeaders`: text
  entry .accessControlMaxAge   `accessControlMaxAge`: text
  entry .accessControlAllowMethods   `accessControlAllowMethods`: text
  entry .accessControlAllowHeaders   `accessControlAllowHeaders`: text
  entry .acceptPatch   `acceptPatch`: text
  entry .acceptRanges   `acceptRanges`: text
  entry .age   `age`: text
  entry .allow   `allow`: text
  entry .altSvc   `altSvc`: text
  entry .contentDisposition   `contentDisposition`: text
  entry .contentLanguage   `contentLanguage`: text
  entry .contentLocation   `contentLocation`: text
  entry .contentRange   `contentRange`: text
  entry .deltaBase   `deltaBase`: text
  entry .eTag   `eTag`: text
  entry .expires   `expires`: text
  entry .im   `im`: text
  entry .lastModified   `lastModified`: text
  entry .link   `link`: text
  entry .location   `location`: text
  entry .p3p   `p3p`: text
  entry .preferenceApplied   `preferenceApplied`: text
  entry .proxyAuthenticate   `proxyAuthenticate`: text
  entry .publicKeyPins   `publicKeyPins`: text
  entry .retryAfter   `retryAfter`: text
  entry .server   `server`: text
  entry .setCookie   `setCookie`: text
  entry .strictTransportSecurity   `strictTransportSecurity`: text
  entry .tk   `tk`: text
  entry .vary   `vary`: text
  entry .wwwAuthenticate   `wwwAuthenticate`: text
  entry .xFrameOptions   `xFrameOptions`: text

entry .Fetchable   what `fetch` and `submit` accept as an endpoint, and the target it sends to
  detail
      A `Fetchable` value yields a request target (its path and query, as text), a `Host`,
      and a `Target` the `Http.Client` is summoned for: an `HttpUrl` targets its
      `Origin["http" | "https"]`, and a `DomainSocket.Endpoint` (with
      `domainSocketFetchable`, in `telekinesis.jvm`) targets its socket. The companion's
      `.httpUrl` covers every `HttpUrl`, including the scheme-refined types `url"…"` literals
      have.
  refer telekinesis.fetch
  refer telekinesis.Http.Client
  entry .httpUrl   any `HttpUrl` is fetchable, targeting its origin
      The request target is the URL's path and query, the host its hostname; a URL with no
      hostname is a defect.
  entry #target   the `Target` of an endpoint
  entry #text   the request target text of an endpoint
  entry #hostname   the `Host` of an endpoint

entry .Parameter   one named field of a request's query or form body, read as a type
  detail
      `Parameter[Int](t"page")` names a field and the type it decodes to and encodes from.
      With an `Http.Request` in scope, `page()` is its value, decoded from the request's
      `query` (URL parameters and a form-encoded body alike), raising `Query.Error` if it is
      absent or malformed; `page(3)` is the `Query` fragment `page=3`; and `case page(n) =>`
      matches a request which has it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      val page = Parameter[Int](t"page")
      page(3).show                       // t"page=3"

      given Http.Request = Http.Request.parse(Chain(t"GET /?page=7 HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: x\r\n\r\n".in[Data]))
      page()                             // 7
      ```

      The value type must be `Decodable in Query` and `Encodable in Query`, which every
      primitive and every product of them is.
  refer telekinesis.Submission
  refer telekinesis.Http.Request
  entry .apply   `Parameter[value](name)`: a parameter of a name and a type
  entry #name   the field's name
  entry #decode   the value from a `Query`
  entry #encode   a `Query` from a value
  entry #apply   `parameter(value)` encodes a value; `parameter()` reads it from the request in scope
      Reading needs an `Http.Request` given, a `Tactic[Query.Error]` for an absent or
      malformed field, and a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` for a form body cut short (reported
      as the field's absence).
  entry #unapply   `case parameter(value) =>` over an `Http.Request`: matches when the field decodes

entry .Postable   how a value becomes a request body: its media type and its bytes
  detail
      A `Postable` value can be the payload of `submit`: the typeclass gives its `MediaType`,
      which becomes the `Content-Type` header, and a fresh `Stream[Data] over Credit` of its
      bytes each time it is asked (a body may be sent again after a redirect, and sampled
      for logging). The companion covers `Text` (`text/plain`, encoded by the `Codepage` in
      scope), `Data` and a `Data & Sized[n]` (`application/octet-stream`), `Chain[Text]` and
      `Chain[Data]` (streamed, `application/octet-stream`), `Query`
      (`application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, a form submission), `Unit` (an empty
      `text/plain` body) and any type `Abstractable across HttpStreams` to a media type and
      stream; the data-format libraries add `postables.jsonPostable`, `xmlPostable`,
      `yamlPostable`, `cborPostable`, `protobufPostable` and gesticulate's multipart.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      url"https://example.com/submit".submit(Http.Post)(t"Hello world")
      url"https://example.com/form".submit()(Query(List(t"name" -> t"Romeo")))
      ```

      A custom instance is `Postable(mediaType, streamer)`, the streamer a function from the
      value to a stream; the instance retains what the streamer captures.
  refer telekinesis.submit
  refer telekinesis.Servable
  entry .Streamer   the function from a value to a fresh body stream, as a single-method trait
    detail
        Named rather than a function type so that its `stream` method may mint a fresh
        scoped stream; a lambda converts to it at the call site.
    entry #stream   a fresh stream of a value's bytes
  entry .apply   `Postable(mediaType, streamer)`: an instance from a media type and a streamer
  entry .text   `Text` posts as `text/plain`, encoded by the `Codepage` in scope
  entry .textStream   a `Chain[Text]` posts as `application/octet-stream`, each block encoded
  entry .unit   `Unit` posts as an empty `text/plain` body
  entry .data   `Data` posts as `application/octet-stream`
  entry .sizedData   a `Data & Sized[length]` posts as `Data` does
  entry .byteStream   a `Chain[Data]` posts as `application/octet-stream`, streamed
  entry .query   a `Query` posts as `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, a form submission
  entry .dataStream   any value `Abstractable across HttpStreams` posts with the media type and stream it abstracts to
      Needs a `Tactic[MediaType.Error]`, raised when the abstracted media type text does
      not parse; the instance retains it.
  entry #mediaType   the media type of a value's body
  entry #stream   a fresh stream of a value's body
  entry #preview   a hex sample of the first block of the body, for logging
      At most one bounded refill, so a large body is never drained to log it; a sample
      longer than 128 bytes ends in `...`.

entry .Receivable   how a response is read as a type: `response.receive[T]`
  detail
      A `Receivable` reads an `Http.Response` to a value. Every instance built with
      `Receivable(reader)` first checks the status: a response outside the `Successful`
      category raises `Http.Error` with its status and headers, and only then is the body
      stream handed to the reader. The companion reads `Text` (the whole body, UTF-8), any
      type `Aggregable by Data` (`Data`, a `Json`, a `Chain[Data]`, …), any type
      `Instantiable across HttpRequests from Text`, and `Http.Status` — which, alone, never
      raises, since it reads no body. The `Instantiable` instance, `instantiable`, is inherited
      from the trait `Receivable2`, at lower priority than the companion's own.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import charsets.utf8Charset

      val response = url"https://example.com/".fetch()
      response.receive[Http.Status]
      response.receive[Text]
      ```

      A body is read once: the stream is a single-owner endpoint over the connection, so a
      second `receive[Text]` on the same response finds it drained. `#map` derives a reader
      for another type from one already in hand.
  refer telekinesis.Http.Response
  refer telekinesis.Http.Error
  entry .Reader   the function from a body stream to a result, as a single-method trait
    detail
        Named rather than a function type so that it may take the capability-typed stream;
        a lambda converts to it at the call site.
    entry #read   the result from a body stream
  entry .apply   `Receivable(reader)`: an instance which checks the status, then reads the body
      Needs a `Tactic[Http.Error]` and a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`, which the instance
      retains along with the reader.
  entry .text   reads the body as `Text`, decoded as UTF-8
  entry .streamable   reads the body through any `Aggregable by Data`: `Data`, a `Json`, a stream
  entry .httpStatus   `receive[Http.Status]`: the status, reading no body and never raising
  entry #read   reads a response, which may be one whose body retains the live connection
  entry #map   a `Receivable` for another type, by a function on this one's result

entry .Receivable2   the lower-priority `Receivable` instance, mixed into the companion
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: holds `instantiable`, the fallback which reads any type
      `Instantiable across HttpRequests from Text` from the body's UTF-8 text, at lower
      priority than `Receivable.text` and `Receivable.streamable`.
  entry #instantiable   reads the body as text and instantiates any `Instantiable across HttpRequests from Text`

entry .Redirects   the namespace of `Redirects.Disabled`, the marker which turns off redirect following
  detail
      Bringing a `Redirects.Disabled` into scope selects `Http.Client.httpStrict`, which
      returns a 3xx response unchanged rather than following it. The given which does so
      is `httpRedirections.doNotFollowRedirects`, imported by name.
  refer telekinesis.httpRedirections
  entry .Disabled   the marker class; an instance in scope disables redirect following

entry .Servable   how a value becomes a response: `Http.Response(value)`, or returning it from a handler
  detail
      A `Servable` value is served as an `Http.Response` with its media type in a
      `content-type` header and its bytes as the body. The companion covers `Text`
      (`text/plain`, encoded by the `Codepage` in scope), `Data` and a `Data & Sized[n]`
      (`application/octet-stream`), gesticulate's `Content` (a media type and a stream), any
      type `Abstractable across HttpStreams`, and — through `.media` — any type with a
      `Media` instance and an `Encodable in Data` or a `Streamable by Data` or `by Text`;
      the data-format libraries add `servables.jsonServable`, `xmlServable`,
      `yamlServable`, `cborServable` and `protobufServable`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val response: Http.Response = Http.Response(t"hello")
      response.headers.contentType     // List(media"text/plain")
      ```

      A custom instance is `Servable(mediaType)(value => body)` or, when the media type
      depends on the value, `Servable(value => mediaType)(value => body)`. A streaming `Text`
      body served through `.media` runs the `Codepage` as a stage of the stream, which a
      server must consume block by block (`sweep`), never `memoize`, on the serving path; a
      `Data` body (`Http.Body.Fixed`) avoids the question.
  refer telekinesis.Postable
  refer telekinesis.Http.Response
  entry .apply   `Servable(mediaType)(value => body)`: an instance from a media type and a body function
      The media type may be a constant (its header is rendered once) or a function of the
      value. The instance retains what the functions capture.
  entry .content   gesticulate's `Content` serves with its own media type and stream
  entry .bytes   any value `Abstractable across HttpStreams` serves with the media type and stream it abstracts to
      A media type text which does not parse is the `Abstractable` instance's defect, and
      panics.
  entry .data   `Data` serves as `application/octet-stream`, fixed
  entry .sizedData   a `Data & Sized[length]` serves as `Data` does
  entry .text   `Text` serves as `text/plain`, fixed, encoded by the `Codepage` in scope
  entry .media   any `Media` type serves with its media type, from its `Encodable in Data` or a `Streamable`
      An inline given which picks, at the summoning site, an `Encodable in Data` (a fixed
      body), a `Streamable by Data over Credit` (a streamed body) or a `Streamable by Text
      over Credit` (streamed through the `Codepage` and `Buffering` in scope), in that
      order.
  entry #serve   the response for a value
  entry #contramap   a `Servable` for another type, by a function to this one

entry .Session   a session key: the identity a session cookie carries across requests
  detail
      `Session(key)` is the value a `Cookie[Session]` stores in the browser and reads back,
      and the key a `Context` keeps per-session state under. It encodes as its bare key and
      inspects as `Session(t"a3f1")`, so the two are told apart. `Findable`, so it can be
      summoned as a contextual value inside `Cookie.session`.
  refer telekinesis.Context
  refer telekinesis.Cookie
  entry .encodable   a session encodes as its key
  entry .decodable   a session decodes from its key
  entry .inspectable   renders as `Session(t"key")`
  entry #key   the session's key

entry .Submission   a form's state: not yet submitted, or submitted with a query to decode
  detail
      `Submission[content](query)` holds the `Query` a form was submitted with, or `Unset`
      when the form is being shown for the first time. `#value` decodes it to the form's
      type, `#valid` says whether it decodes, and `#fresh`/`#submitted` say which state it is
      in. With `telekinesis.forms`, `submission.form(…)` renders it as HTML.
  refer telekinesis.form
  refer telekinesis.Orchestrate
  entry #query   the submitted `Query`, or `Unset`
  entry #fresh   whether the form has not been submitted
  entry #submitted   whether the form has been submitted
  entry #optional   the decoded value, or `Unset` if absent or invalid
  entry #valid   whether the submitted query decodes to the form's type
  entry #value   the decoded value, or `Unset` if not submitted; a malformed query raises

entry .TransferEncoding   the `Transfer-Encoding` header's values
  detail
      An enum: `Chunked`, `Compress`, `Deflate` and `Gzip`, each encoding as its lower-case
      name. The type the `transferEncoding` header argument takes.
  entry .encodable   encodes a transfer encoding as its lower-case name

entry .internal   the macro implementations behind `fetch`, `submit` and `Http.Response(…)`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home.
  entry .expand   sorts a call's arguments into the method, the status and the encoded headers
      Each named argument is checked against the `Directive` of its name and each unnamed
      one against the unique `Directive` of its type; a method or status given twice, or a
      value which fits no header, is a compile error.
  entry .submit   the macro behind `Http.Submit#apply`: builds the request with its `Content-Type` and sends it
  entry .fetch   the macro behind `Http.Fetch#apply`: builds the bodiless request and sends it
  entry .response   the macro behind `Http.Response(…)`: a served value, or a `Protoresponse`

entry .fetch   `url.fetch(…)`: a `GET` (or other bodiless) request to a URL, with typed headers
  detail
      An extension on any `Fetchable` endpoint — an `HttpUrl`, or a `DomainSocket.Endpoint`
      on the JVM — which returns an `Http.Fetch`; its `apply` is a macro which takes an
      optional `Http.Method` (default `Http.Get`) and any headers, builds the request, and
      sends it through the `Http.Client` for the endpoint's target. Headers are named
      arguments checked by the `Directive` of their name, or unnamed values whose type
      identifies the header. The call needs an `Online` (`internetAccess.online`), an
      `Http.Event is Loggable` (`logging.silentLogging`), an `Http.Backend` from
      `httpBackends`, and tactics for `Connect.Error` and, on `receive`, `Http.Error`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      frame
        label A bare fetch: the status alone
        code
            import soundness.*
            import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
            import internetAccess.online
            import logging.silentLogging
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            url"https://example.com/".fetch().status
      frame
        label A header, named and checked: `accept` takes a `MediaType`
        code
            import soundness.*
            import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
            import internetAccess.online
            import logging.silentLogging
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            url"https://example.com/".fetch(accept = media"application/json").status
      frame
        label A second header: `authorization` takes an `Auth`
        code
            import soundness.*
            import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
            import internetAccess.online
            import logging.silentLogging
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            url"https://example.com/".fetch(accept = media"application/json", authorization = Auth.Bearer(t"token")).status
      frame
        label A method other than `GET`, given positionally
        code
            import soundness.*
            import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
            import internetAccess.online
            import logging.silentLogging
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            url"https://example.com/".fetch(Http.Head, accept = media"application/json", authorization = Auth.Bearer(t"token")).status
      frame
        label The body read as a type
        code
            import soundness.*
            import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
            import internetAccess.online
            import logging.silentLogging
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import charsets.utf8Charset

            url"https://example.com/".fetch(accept = media"application/json", authorization = Auth.Bearer(t"token")).receive[Text]
      ##
      ```

      Redirects are followed, up to ten, unless `httpRedirections.doNotFollowRedirects` is
      imported; a response outside the success range is returned as it is, and raises
      `Http.Error` only when its body is read with `receive`. A request which cannot reach
      the server raises `Connect.Error`.
  refer telekinesis.submit
  refer telekinesis.Http.Fetch
  refer telekinesis.Http.Client
  refer telekinesis.Directive

entry .submit   `url.submit(…)(payload)`: a request with a body, the body a typed `Postable` value
  detail
      An extension on any `Fetchable` endpoint which returns an `Http.Submit`; its `apply`
      takes the method (default `Http.Post`) and headers in a first argument list and the
      payload in a second. The payload is any value with a `Postable` — `Text`, `Data`, a
      `Query` (a form submission), a `Json` with `postables.jsonPostable` — and its media
      type becomes the `Content-Type` header, so nothing is set by hand to match.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      url"https://example.com/submit".submit(Http.Post, accept = media"application/json")(t"Hello world")
      url"https://example.com/items".submit(Http.Put)(t"Hello world")
      url"https://example.com/submit".submit()(t"Hello world")
      ```

      Needs the same contextual values as `fetch`: an `Online`, an `Http.Event is
      Loggable`, an `Http.Backend` and a `Tactic[Connect.Error]`, plus the `Postable`'s own
      (a `Codepage` for `Text`).
  refer telekinesis.fetch
  refer telekinesis.Postable
  refer telekinesis.Http.Submit

entry .query   `value.query`: a value as a `Query`; `url.query(query)`: a URL with parameters added
    Two extensions share the name. `value.query` encodes any value `Encodable in Query` —
    a case class, by generic derivation, nested fields joined with dots — as a `Query`:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
    case class Couple(first: Person, second: Person)

    Person(t"Romeo", 16).query.show
    // t"name=Romeo&age=16"
    Couple(Person(t"Romeo", 16), Person(t"Juliet", 13)).query.show
    // t"first.name=Romeo&first.age=16&second.name=Juliet&second.age=13"
    ```

    `url.query(query)` is an `HttpUrl` with the query's parameters appended to any it
    already has, for building a request URL from typed parameters:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    url"https://example.com/search".query(Query(List(t"q" -> t"soundness"))).show
    ```

entry .queryParameters   the given which lets a `Query` be built with arbitrary field names
  detail
      `Query.make(key = value, …)` checks each named argument against a `key is Parametric
      to value` given, which pins the name and type of each field a query may have. The
      `arbitraryQueryParameter` given, imported by name, accepts any name and any type, so a
      query can be built ad hoc, with nested products flattened by generic derivation.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import queryParameters.arbitraryQueryParameter

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      Query.make(person = Person(t"Iago", 28)).show
      // t"person.name=Iago&person.age=28"
      ```
  entry .arbitraryQueryParameter   any label is a query parameter of any type

entry .httpRedirections   the redirect policy, as a given to import by name
  detail
      Without any import the `Http.Client` follows `301`, `302`, `303`, `307` and `308`
      responses up to the `Http.Redirection` limit, ten by default. Importing
      `doNotFollowRedirects` brings a `Redirects.Disabled` into scope, which selects
      `Http.Client.httpStrict` instead: a redirect response is then returned as it is, and
      reading its body raises `Http.Error` with the 3xx status.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import httpRedirections.doNotFollowRedirects
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import charsets.utf8Charset

      capture[Http.Error](url"https://example.com/old".fetch().receive[Text]).status  // Http.Found
      ```
  refer telekinesis.Http.Redirection
  refer telekinesis.Http.Client
  entry .doNotFollowRedirects   a `Redirects.Disabled`: 3xx responses are returned, not followed

entry .construables   the given which construes `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` as a `Query`
  detail
      A `Construable` maps a media type to the Scala type a response of that type is read
      as, which apoplexy's generated API clients use to decode a response body. Importing
      `formConstruable` by name reads a form-encoded body as a `Query`.
  entry .formConstruable   `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` construes to `Query`

entry .httpBackends   the HTTP transports, each an `Http.Backend` given to import by name
  detail
      Which wire carries a request is decided by the `Http.Backend` in scope, and the choice
      is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these in, and one is imported
      by name. `javaNetHttp` uses the JVM's own `java.net.http`; `soundnessHttp` speaks
      Soundness's wire codecs directly over coaxial's sockets, with no `java.net.http`
      involved, on the JVM and Scala Native; `wasiHttp` calls `wasi:http` from a Wasm
      component. Everything above the backend — redirects, logging, the typed headers and
      bodies — is the same whichever is chosen.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      alternative `javaNetHttp`
        code
            import soundness.*
            import internetAccess.online
            import logging.silentLogging
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            locally:
              import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
              url"https://example.com/".fetch().status   // Http.Ok
      alternative `soundnessHttp`
        code
            import soundness.*
            import internetAccess.online
            import logging.silentLogging
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets

            locally:
              import httpBackends.soundnessHttp
              url"https://example.com/".fetch().status   // Http.Ok
      alternative No redirects
        code
            import soundness.*
            import internetAccess.online
            import logging.silentLogging
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets

            locally:
              import httpBackends.soundnessHttp
              import httpRedirections.doNotFollowRedirects
              url"https://example.com/".fetch().status   // Http.Ok, or the 3xx itself
      ##
      ```
  refer telekinesis.Http.Backend
  refer telekinesis.Http.Client
  entry .javaNetHttp   the JVM transport over `java.net.http`
      In `telekinesis.jvm`. Needs a `TlsAcceptance` in scope (coaxial's: the strict default
      verifies hostnames and chains; `TlsAcceptance().permitSelfSigned` and its siblings
      relax it under a `cryptoPermits` import), and builds one JDK client per distinct
      acceptance, cached. Redirect following is left to `Http.Client`, so the
      `Http.Redirection` limit is honoured exactly. A request to a cleartext `http://`
      origin is sent as HTTP/1.1, never offering the `h2c` upgrade; over `https`, ALPN may
      negotiate HTTP/2 inside the JDK. The body is published lazily from a fresh stream on
      each subscription, so a large body is never held whole in memory. The JDK's
      exceptions are classified into `Connect.Error` reasons: `UnknownHostException` is
      `Dns`, a refused or timed-out connection `Refused` or `Timeout`, and the SSL
      exceptions `Ssl(Handshake)`, `Ssl(Protocol)`, `Ssl(Peer)` or `Ssl(Key)`.
  entry .soundnessHttp   the transport speaking telekinesis's wire codecs over coaxial's sockets
      In `telekinesis.sockets`, on every platform with a `Socket.Backend`. Needs an
      `Online`, a `Socket.Backend` (`socketBackends.javaBaseSockets` on the JVM), an
      `Every[Socket.Option.Tcp]`, a `Buffering` and a `Tls`. Plaintext `http` is HTTP/1.1
      over a raw TCP socket with keep-alive: when a response's extent was explicit (a framed
      body, or none) and neither side asked to close, its connection returns to a
      per-origin pool (at most eight idle per origin) for the next request; a pooled socket
      the server has since closed is discovered on use and replaced by a single retry on a
      fresh connection. For `https` on the JVM, ALPN offers `h2` then `http/1.1` during the
      handshake, and the exchange is driven by the HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1 driver the peer
      selected, over the same socket, one connection per request; on Scala Native it is one
      HTTP/1.1 exchange over coaxial's OpenSSL connection, with no ALPN and no HTTP/2.
      Framed bodies are drained eagerly before a connection is surrendered or closed, so
      responses do not stream yet, and `101` upgrades are refused (the upgraded stream
      would never end) as `Connect.Error(Unknown)`. A `Socket.Error` maps onto
      `Connect.Error` by reason, and a malformed response is `Unknown`.
  entry .wasiHttp   the transport over `wasi:http/outgoing-handler`, for a Wasm component
      In `telekinesis.wasi`. An `inline` given: the `wasi:http/types` calls expand at the
      site which summons it, which must have `wasiApis.wasiHttpApi` in scope, and only
      materialise in code compiled for a Wasm target. The request is assembled from a
      `fields` resource and an `outgoing-request`, with an `outgoing-body` when the method
      carries one; the response is awaited through its pollable and its body read whole
      from the `input-stream`, so the result is a `Fixed` body. Any failure of the host
      call is `Connect.Error(Unknown)`.

entry .Orchestrate   a form round-trip: render it, or on `POST` decode, validate and act
  detail
      An `Orchestrate[value, result]` (built with `orchestrate`) handles both halves of a
      form: on any request but a `POST` it renders the form, pre-filled with `initial` if
      given; on a `POST` it decodes the request's query to `value`, and if that fails runs
      the caller's validation to annotate the fields. In both cases the caller's `process`
      function receives a `form` renderer (`Text => Html of Flow`, taking the submit
      button's label) and the `Optional[value]` — `Unset` when the form is to be shown
      again, the value when it decoded — and decides what to respond. It is a capability,
      since it retains the caller's function. In `telekinesis.forms`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Signup(name: Text, email: Text)

      def handle(using Http.Request, Formulation): Http.Response =
        orchestrate[Signup](): value =>
          value.lay(Http.Response(form(t"Sign up"))): signup =>
            Http.Response(t"Welcome, ${signup.name}")
        . otherwise(Validation())
      ```
  refer telekinesis.orchestrate
  refer telekinesis.Submission
  entry #otherwise   runs the round-trip with a validation to apply when a `POST` does not decode
      `otherwise(validate)` needs a `Formulation` (legerdemain's form styling), the
      value's `Formulaic` (how its fields render) and `Decodable in Query`, the
      `Http.Request` in scope, and tactics for `Query.Error` and `Truncation.Error`. The
      validation is a context function over the submitted `Query`.

entry .orchestrate   `orchestrate[value](initial)(render)`: builds an `Orchestrate` for a form type
    The `render` function receives the form renderer as a contextual value and the
    `Optional[value]` — the decoded submission, or `Unset` when the form is to be shown —
    and produces the result; `.otherwise(validation)` on the result then runs it. The
    result retains what `render` captures. In `telekinesis.forms`.

entry .form   `submission.form(…)`: a `Submission` rendered as an HTML form
    An extension on `Submission[value]`, in `telekinesis.forms` (so that the core model does
    not depend on honeycomb and legerdemain's widgets): renders the form's fields through
    the value's `Formulaic` and the `Formulation` in scope, pre-filled from the submitted
    query, with the given `submit` button label and any `Validation` messages against the
    fields. Returns an `Html of Flow`.

entry .requestTransmissible   an `Http.Request` is transmitted over a raw socket as its HTTP/1.1 wire form
    The coaxial `Transmissible` given, in `telekinesis.jvm`, built on
    `Http.Request.serialize`; what the domain-socket client sends with `socket.transmit(request)`.
    It is a top-level given, so it must be imported by name
    (`import soundness.requestTransmissible`), and needs a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`.

entry .domainSocketFetchable   a `DomainSocket.Endpoint` is fetchable: HTTP/1.1 straight over a Unix domain socket
    In `telekinesis.jvm`, for an API served on a local socket such as the Docker daemon's.
    The request target is the endpoint's path, the `Host` is `localhost`, and the target the
    client is summoned for is the `DomainSocket`. A top-level given, imported by name.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.{domainSocketFetchable, domainSocketHttpClient, requestTransmissible}
    import internetAccess.online
    import logging.silentLogging
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import charsets.utf8Charset

    val docker = DomainSocket(t"/var/run/docker.sock")
    docker.at(t"/version").fetch().receive[Text]
    ```

entry .domainSocketHttpClient   the `Http.Client onto DomainSocket`: one request, one response, over the socket
    In `telekinesis.jvm`. Serialises the request with `requestTransmissible`, parses the
    reply with `Http.Response.parse`, and does not follow redirects. Needs a
    `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` and a `Tactic[Connect.Error]`: a `Socket.Error` is mapped
    onto `Connect.Error` by reason, and a malformed response is
    `Connect.Error(Connect.Error.Reason.Unknown)`. A top-level given, imported by name.

entry .WasiHttpApi   the WIT interface type of `wasi:http`, as xenophile sees it
    `Interface in Wit at "/telekinesis/http.wit"`: the WIT definitions bundled with
    `telekinesis.wasi`, against which `httpBackends.wasiHttp` and `WasiHttpServer` are
    typechecked, and which their `call`s consult at the expansion site for module ids,
    resource methods and parameter types.

entry .wasiApis   the `wasi:http` interface, as a given to import by name
  detail
      `wasiHttpApi` must be in scope wherever `httpBackends.wasiHttp` is summoned or
      `WasiHttpServer.handle` is called, since both are `inline` and resolve their WIT calls
      at that site. It merges with the umbrella's `wasiApis` package, beside the other
      libraries' WASI interfaces.
  entry .wasiHttpApi   the `WasiHttpApi` interface, read from the bundled `http.wit` resource

entry .WasiHttpServer   serves HTTP from a Wasm component: `wasi:http/incoming-handler` to a Soundness handler
  detail
      The bridge from the component's exported `handle` function to a
      `Http.Request => Http.Response`. The application supplies a small `@WitExport` shim,
      the one piece which must exist, annotated, in the Wasm-compiled application, and
      passes the two facade instances through untyped; `WasiHttpServer.handle` does the
      rest: unmarshals the request (its method, path, headers and whole body; the host is
      `Localhost`, since a component is not addressed by hostname), calls the handler,
      marshals the response's status, headers and body, and sets the outparam before the
      body is written so the host can stream it. `inline`, so the WIT calls expand at the
      call site, which needs `wasiApis.wasiHttpApi` in scope.
  refer telekinesis.wasiApis
  entry .handle   `WasiHttpServer.handle(request, responseOut)(handler)`: answers one incoming request
      The two untyped facades are the `incoming-request` and `response-outparam` the host
      passed to the export. A response body the handler cuts short traps the component, as
      any other failure of the export does.

entry .Http2   the HTTP/2 engine: frames, HPACK-coded headers, and the client and server roles of a multiplexed connection
  detail
      `Http2` groups the HTTP/2 (RFC 7540) vocabulary under one name, as `Http` groups the
      transport-agnostic model: a `Frame` and its `FrameType`, `Flags`, `ErrorCode`, `SettingId`
      and `Setting`; the two roles of a connection, `Connection` (the client, which opens
      streams) and `ServerConnection` (the server, which accepts them); the `Stream` both roles
      share; the `Endpoint` a cleartext h2c client connects to, with its `Client` given and
      `sessional` session; and the `Error` and `Event` the engine raises and logs.

      A connection multiplexes many request streams over one persistent `Duplex`. A writer
      daemon drains an outbound queue of frames to the socket, so every write is serialised
      without a lock, and a reader daemon decodes inbound frames and dispatches them by stream
      id; both run under the `Monitor` of an enclosing `supervise`, which is why a connection
      can only be made inside one. The handshake is `start()`: each side's `SETTINGS` and a
      `WINDOW_UPDATE` raising the connection window to its receive budget. Flow control is
      honoured in both directions — a body larger than the peer's window parks the sending
      fiber until a `WINDOW_UPDATE` grants more, and an inbound body the application has not
      drained holds the peer at the advertised window — and the frame types the stack does not
      act on (`PRIORITY`, `PUSH_PROMISE`, extensions) are decoded only to be ignored, as the RFC
      requires, rather than failing the connection.

      The engine is the HTTP/2 driver of both sides of Soundness's HTTP: the `https` client
      backend uses `Connection` on any TLS connection whose ALPN negotiation selects `h2`, and
      a TLS `SocketServer` hands a connection which negotiated `h2` to `ServerConnection`. The
      cleartext form, h2c with prior knowledge (no upgrade, no TLS), is reached through an
      `Http2.Endpoint`, which is the transport gRPC runs over.

      The pipeline from a request to the bytes on the wire is a chain of these values, each a
      step a program may take by hand:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The header list of a request, pseudo-headers first
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val request =
              Http.Request(Http.Get, 2.0, t"example.com".as[Host], t"/", Nil, () => Http.emptyBody())

            val entries: List[Hpack.Entry] = PseudoHeaders.request(request, t"https", t"example.com")
      frame
        label Encoded as an HPACK header block
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val request =
              Http.Request(Http.Get, 2.0, t"example.com".as[Host], t"/", Nil, () => Http.emptyBody())

            val entries: List[Hpack.Entry] = PseudoHeaders.request(request, t"https", t"example.com")
            val block: Data = Hpack().encode(entries)
      frame
        label Carried by a HEADERS frame on stream 1, which also ends the stream
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val request =
              Http.Request(Http.Get, 2.0, t"example.com".as[Host], t"/", Nil, () => Http.emptyBody())

            val entries: List[Hpack.Entry] = PseudoHeaders.request(request, t"https", t"example.com")
            val block: Data = Hpack().encode(entries)
            val frame = Http2.Frame.Headers(1, block, endStream = true, endHeaders = true)
      frame
        label Serialised: a nine-byte frame header, then the block
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val request =
              Http.Request(Http.Get, 2.0, t"example.com".as[Host], t"/", Nil, () => Http.emptyBody())

            val entries: List[Hpack.Entry] = PseudoHeaders.request(request, t"https", t"example.com")
            val block: Data = Hpack().encode(entries)
            val frame = Http2.Frame.Headers(1, block, endStream = true, endHeaders = true)
            val wire: Data = frame.serialize
      ##
      ```

      `Connection#fetch` does all of this for an `Http.Request`, and `FrameReader` and
      `Hpack#decode` are the inverse on the receiving side. Server push is not supported (the
      client's `SETTINGS` disable it), and a `PUSH_PROMISE` from a peer is ignored.
  refer telekinesis.Http2.Connection
  refer telekinesis.Http2.ServerConnection
  refer telekinesis.Http2.Endpoint
  refer telekinesis.Hpack
  refer telekinesis.FrameReader
  refer telekinesis.PseudoHeaders
  entry .Connection   the client role: a multiplexed HTTP/2 connection over a `Duplex`, opening a stream per request
    detail
        `Http2.Connection(duplex)` wraps a persistent `Duplex` — a socket, or one side of an
        in-memory `Duplex.pair()` — as the client end of an HTTP/2 connection, speaking
        cleartext h2c with prior knowledge: it sends the client connection preface and then
        frames, with no upgrade and no TLS of its own (the `https` backend hands it a `Duplex`
        whose TLS handshake has already negotiated `h2`). It needs a `Monitor` and a `Probate`
        in scope, since its reader and writer daemons run under them, so it is made inside a
        `supervise` block, and `#start` must be called before the first request.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import threads.virtualThreads
        import probates.cancelProbate
        import logging.silentLogging

        def exchange(duplex: Duplex, request: Http.Request): Http.Status = supervise:
          val connection = Http2.Connection(duplex)
          connection.start()

          try
            val (stream, response) = connection.fetch(request, t"http", t"example.com")
            response.status
          finally connection.close()
        ```

        The second constructor parameter, `window`, is the receive budget the connection
        advertises per stream and for the connection as a whole (1 MiB by default): the peer may
        have at most that many bytes in flight beyond what the application has consumed, which
        is what bounds the memory an unread response body can occupy. Replenishment is driven
        by consumption and batched: a `WINDOW_UPDATE` goes out once half the budget has been
        drained, so a stalled consumer stalls the peer's sender rather than accumulating its
        output. A budget below the RFC default of 65535 leaves the connection window at the
        default, and the stream window is what gates.

        Streams are numbered 1, 3, 5, …, as a client's must be. Concurrent `#fetch` calls
        multiplex on the one connection. A write, parse or HPACK failure in either daemon
        tears the connection down — a pending handshake is unblocked, every open stream is
        ended so nothing awaiting its headers, body or trailers hangs, and the writer exits —
        rather than escaping the daemon or being swallowed; a `GOAWAY` from the peer ends the
        reader and logs `Http2.Event.GoAway`.
    refer telekinesis.Http2.Stream
    refer telekinesis.Http2.Endpoint
    refer telekinesis.Http2.ServerConnection
    entry #start   performs the handshake: sends this side's `SETTINGS` and awaits the peer's
        Sends `SETTINGS` disabling server push and advertising the receive window, raises the
        connection window above the RFC default with a `WINDOW_UPDATE` if the budget is larger,
        and blocks until the peer's own `SETTINGS` frame has arrived (which the reader
        acknowledges). It needs a `Tactic[Async.Error]`, raised if the connection is torn down
        before the handshake completes. Nothing may be sent before it returns.
    entry #request   opens a stream, sends a header block and an optional body, and returns the stream handle
        The frame-level form of a request: allocates the next odd stream id, encodes `headerBlock`
        (whose pseudo-headers must already come first, as `PseudoHeaders.request` arranges) with a
        fresh HPACK encoder, and sends it as a `HEADERS` frame, with `END_STREAM` set when `body`
        is `Unset`. A body is sent as `DATA` frames under the peer's connection and stream
        windows and its maximum frame size, so a large upload parks the calling fiber at window
        exhaustion until the peer's `WINDOW_UPDATE`s grant more. The returned `Http2.Stream` is
        populated by the reader daemon as the response arrives: await `#headers`, consume
        `#body`, then `#trailers`.
    entry #fetch   issues an `Http.Request` and returns its stream and `Http.Response`, blocking only for the response headers
        Builds the header block with `PseudoHeaders.request(request, scheme, authority)` — an
        `Http.Request` carries neither the scheme nor the `:authority`, so both are given here —
        memoizes the request body, opens the stream with `#request`, and awaits the response
        `HEADERS`. The `Http.Response` it returns streams its body lazily from the stream's
        inbound `DATA` frames, so a large response is read as it arrives, and the stream is
        returned beside it so that trailers (gRPC's `grpc-status`, for instance) can be awaited
        after the body: `stream.trailers.await()`.

        It logs `Http2.Event.RequestSent` at `fine`, so an `Http2.Event is Loggable` such as
        `logging.silentLogging` is needed, and it takes a `Tactic[Http2.Error]` (a protocol error
        on this stream, or a response with no valid `:status`), a `Tactic[Async.Error]` (the
        connection torn down while awaiting) and a `Tactic[Truncation.Error]` (a request body
        cut short while being memoized).
    entry #close   sends `GOAWAY`, stops the daemons and closes the `Duplex`
        A clean shutdown: `GOAWAY` with `NoError`, then the outbound queue is stopped, the reader
        and writer are cancelled, and the duplex is closed. A connection made inside a
        `supervise` block should be closed before the block ends, or the block waits on the
        daemons. Closing the client does not stop a `ServerConnection` on the other side of an
        in-memory pair, which must be closed on its own.
  entry .ServerConnection   the server role: accepts the streams a client opens and sends responses back on them
    detail
        `Http2.ServerConnection(duplex)` is the server end of an HTTP/2 connection whose client
        has already been negotiated or is speaking h2c with prior knowledge. Its reader daemon
        first consumes and validates the 24-byte client connection preface (a mismatch is an
        `Http2.Error` with a `Protocol` reason), then decodes frames; every `HEADERS` frame on a
        stream id it has not seen creates a `Stream`, whose `headers` promise is the request's
        header block, and hands it to `#eachStream`; a second `HEADERS` block on a live stream
        is the request's trailers. The writer daemon drains the outbound queue, so
        `#sendHeaders`, `#sendData` and `#sendTrailers` may be called concurrently for
        different streams — frames interleave by design, and each header block is encoded with
        a fresh HPACK encoder, so no encoder state is shared between handlers. It needs a
        `Monitor` and a `Probate`, so it is made inside a `supervise` block, and the `Duplex`
        it takes may capture its I/O capabilities (`Duplex^`), as a socket's does.

        A response is grown on a stream from its header block:

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label A bodiless response: the header block alone ends the stream
          code
              import soundness.*
              import codepages.utf8Codepage

              def respond(server: Http2.ServerConnection, stream: Http2.Stream): Unit =
                val head = List(Hpack.Entry(t":status", t"204"))
                server.sendHeaders(stream.id, head, endStream = true)
        frame
          label A body: the headers stay open, and the last DATA frame ends the stream
          code
              import soundness.*
              import codepages.utf8Codepage

              def respond(server: Http2.ServerConnection, stream: Http2.Stream): Unit =
                val head = List(Hpack.Entry(t":status", t"200"))
                server.sendHeaders(stream.id, head, endStream = false)
                server.sendData(stream.id, t"Hello over h2!".in[Data], endStream = true)
        frame
          label Trailers: headers and body stay open, and the trailing block ends the stream
          code
              import soundness.*
              import codepages.utf8Codepage

              def respond(server: Http2.ServerConnection, stream: Http2.Stream): Unit =
                val head = List(Hpack.Entry(t":status", t"200"))
                server.sendHeaders(stream.id, head, endStream = false)
                server.sendData(stream.id, t"Hello over h2!".in[Data], endStream = false)
                server.sendTrailers(stream.id, List(Hpack.Entry(t"grpc-status", t"0")))
        ##
        ```

        `PseudoHeaders.requestOf` turns an accepted stream's header block and body into an
        `Http.Request`, and `PseudoHeaders.entries` turns an `Http.Response` into the header
        block to send, which is how a TLS `SocketServer` serves an ordinary handler over h2
        with no change to the handler. Two rules for anyone driving the engine directly: a
        flowing response body must be drained to the stream block by block, never memoized,
        since a transforming body (a text encoder's) does not terminate under `memoize`; and
        the connection must be `#close`d before the enclosing `supervise` block returns, or the
        block waits forever on its daemons — closing the client on the other side does not stop
        the server's writer.

        The receive budget (`window`, 1 MiB by default) and its batched replenishment are as
        `Http2.Connection`'s: a request body the handler has not drained holds the client at the
        advertised window. The peer's `SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE` applies to streams opened
        after it arrives; existing streams are not adjusted. Server push is not implemented.
    refer telekinesis.Http2.Stream
    refer telekinesis.PseudoHeaders
    refer telekinesis.Http2.Connection
    entry #start   performs the server side of the handshake: sends `SETTINGS` and awaits the client's
        Sends `SETTINGS` advertising the receive window (a server does not send `ENABLE_PUSH`),
        raises the connection window above the RFC default if the budget is larger, and blocks
        until the client's `SETTINGS` frame has arrived and been acknowledged. It needs a
        `Tactic[Async.Error]`, raised if the connection is torn down first — a bad preface,
        for instance. The client's preface and `SETTINGS` and the server's `SETTINGS` may be
        sent in either order, so `start()` on both sides of an in-memory pair may run
        concurrently.
    entry #eachStream   runs a handler for every stream the client opens, in arrival order, until the connection ends
        Takes each accepted `Http2.Stream` from the queue the reader fills and calls `handler`
        on the reader-driven serve loop, returning when the connection ends (a `GOAWAY`, the
        socket closing, or `#close`). The handler is given the stream as soon as its request
        header block has arrived — the body may still be streaming in — and should spawn a
        fiber of its own for the stream, so that requests multiplexed on the one connection are
        served concurrently rather than one after another on the loop.
    entry #sendHeaders   sends a response header block on a stream, ending the stream if the response has no body
        Encodes `entries` with a fresh HPACK encoder and sends them as one `HEADERS` frame with
        `END_HEADERS` set. `:status` must come first, as `PseudoHeaders.entries` arranges.
        `endStream` is `true` for a bodiless response and `false` when `#sendData` or
        `#sendTrailers` will follow.
    entry #sendData   sends a body as `DATA` frames on a stream, under the peer's flow-control windows
        Splits `payload` into chunks bounded by the connection window, the stream window and the
        peer's `SETTINGS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE`, and blocks the calling fiber at window exhaustion
        until an inbound `WINDOW_UPDATE` grants more, so a response larger than the window
        streams intact without ever exceeding the peer's credit. `endStream` rides the final
        chunk; an empty payload carries no data and is sent at once, its only role being to end
        the stream.
    entry #sendTrailers   sends a trailing header block, which always ends the stream
        A second `HEADERS` block after the body, with `END_STREAM` set — the response's
        trailers, such as gRPC's `grpc-status`. A response with trailers leaves `endStream`
        unset on its `#sendHeaders` and `#sendData`, so that this block is what closes the
        stream. Encoded with a fresh HPACK encoder.
    entry #close   sends `GOAWAY`, stops the serve loop and the daemons, and closes the `Duplex`
        `GOAWAY` with `NoError`, then the outbound queue and the accepted-stream queue are
        stopped (so `#eachStream` returns), the reader and writer are cancelled, and the duplex
        is closed. Call it before the enclosing `supervise` block ends.
  entry .Stream   one HTTP/2 stream's receive side: promises for its header blocks and a flow-controlled body
    detail
        A `Stream` is what a request looks like on the receiving side of a connection, in either
        role: for `Http2.Connection` it is the response to a request the client opened, and for
        `Http2.ServerConnection` it is a request the client sent. It has an `#id`, a promise for
        its first `HEADERS` block (`#headers`: the response headers, or the request's), a
        promise for a second, end-stream block (`#trailers`), and a `#body` fed by `DATA`
        frames. The connection's reader daemon populates them as frames arrive, and resolves
        whatever is still pending — with `Nil`, and by ending the body — when the stream ends,
        is reset by the peer, or the connection is torn down, so nothing awaiting a stream
        hangs.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        def status(stream: Http2.Stream): Optional[Text] =
          val head: List[Hpack.Entry] = stream.headers.await()
          val body: Data = stream.body.stream.memoize
          stream.trailers.await().seek(_.name == t"grpc-status").let(_.value)
        ```

        The body is a relay bounded by protocol credit rather than by a buffer size: the peer
        may have at most the connection's advertised window in flight beyond what the consumer
        has drained, and draining the body is what replenishes the window. A stream is made by
        the connection, never by hand; `#acceptHeaders`, `#acceptData` and `#end` are the
        reader daemon's entry points.
    refer telekinesis.Http2.Connection
    refer telekinesis.Http2.ServerConnection
    refer telekinesis.Hpack.Entry
    entry .Body   the inbound body of a stream: a relay of `DATA` payloads, metered by flow control
      detail
          A `Body` collects the payload of each `DATA` frame on its stream and serves them as a
          stream of byte blocks. Its buffering is unbounded in structure but bounded by protocol
          credit — the peer cannot send more than the advertised window without a
          `WINDOW_UPDATE` — and the update is sent only as the consumer skips past records, so
          an unread body holds the peer at the window rather than accumulating in memory. It is
          made by `Http2.Stream`, with the connection's consumption callback.
      entry #stream   the body as a stream of byte blocks, whose consumption replenishes the peer's window
          A `zephyrine.Stream` of `Data` records over `Credit`, needing a `Buffering` in scope.
          Each record the consumer skips past is counted and reported to the connection, which
          batches the counts into `WINDOW_UPDATE`s at the half-window threshold. Each call
          re-lends the same underlying relay, so a second read resumes where the first stopped;
          `memoize` on it gathers the whole body as one `Data`.
    entry #id   the stream's identifier: odd for a client-opened stream, and `0` is never a stream
    entry #headers   a promise for the first header block: the response's headers, or the request's
        Resolved by the reader daemon on the stream's first `HEADERS` frame, as the decoded
        `Hpack.Entry` list with its pseudo-headers (`:status`, or `:method`, `:scheme`,
        `:authority` and `:path`) ahead of the regular fields. If the stream ends without one, it
        is resolved with `Nil`. `await()` needs a `Tactic[Async.Error]`.
    entry #trailers   a promise for a second, end-stream header block: the trailers, or `Nil` if there are none
        Resolved on a second `HEADERS` block on the stream, which the protocol requires to end
        the stream — gRPC's `grpc-status` arrives this way — and with `Nil` when the stream ends
        with no second block, so awaiting it after the body is always safe. `await()` needs a
        `Tactic[Async.Error]`.
    entry #body   the stream's inbound body, an `Http2.Stream.Body`
    entry #acceptHeaders   records an inbound header block: the first as the headers, any later one as the trailers
        Called by the connection's reader daemon with a decoded block. Not for application use.
    entry #acceptData   appends an inbound `DATA` payload to the body
        Called by the connection's reader daemon. Not for application use.
    entry #end   closes the receive side, resolving anything still pending so no awaiter hangs
        An unresolved `#headers` or `#trailers` is resolved with `Nil`, and the body is stopped.
        Called by the connection on `END_STREAM`, `RST_STREAM`, `GOAWAY` and tear-down.
  entry .Endpoint   a cleartext h2c endpoint: a connectable address and the `:authority` to send, for `Http.Client` and sessions
    detail
        `Http2.Endpoint(endpoint, authority)` names a server to speak HTTP/2 to in the clear,
        with prior knowledge: `endpoint` is any value which is `Connectable` (and `Showable`) —
        a coaxial `Endpoint(host, port)` over TCP, a `DomainSocket`, or a test's loopback — and
        `authority` is the `:authority` pseudo-header every request carries, which the address
        alone does not say. It is the `Target` of the HTTP/2 `Http.Client` given (`Client.http2`),
        where telekinesis's HTTP/1.1 client targets a URL, and the subject of `.sessional`, which
        lends one live connection to a scope.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import threads.virtualThreads
        import probates.cancelProbate
        import logging.silentLogging
        import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets

        supervise:
          val endpoint = Http2.Endpoint(Endpoint(t"localhost", Port[Tcp](50051)), t"localhost")

          endpoint.session: connection ?=>
            val request =
              Http.Request(Http.Get, 2.0, t"localhost".as[Host], t"/", Nil, () => Http.emptyBody())

            val (stream, response) = connection.fetch(request, t"http", t"localhost")
            response.body.stream.memoize.utf8
        ```

        This is the transport gRPC runs over: a `Grpc.Channel` is opened on an `Http2.Endpoint`.
    refer telekinesis.Http2.Connection
    refer telekinesis.Http2.Client
    refer telekinesis.Http2.EndpointSessional
    entry #endpoint   the connectable address
    entry #authority   the `:authority` pseudo-header sent on every request, such as `localhost:50051`
    entry #connect   opens a multiplexed connection which lives until the enclosing `supervise` scope ends
        Connects the address, makes an `Http2.Connection` over the `Duplex`, completes its
        handshake and returns it. The connection runs on a daemon which holds the duplex open,
        parked until the scope is torn down, so the response bodies it streams lazily stay
        readable for the scope's lifetime rather than being closed per request; nothing closes
        it before then. It needs a `Monitor` and a `Probate` (so it is called inside
        `supervise`), a `Tactic[Socket.Error]` for an address which cannot be connected, and a
        `Tactic[Async.Error]` for a connection which is cancelled before it is ready. The
        result retains the monitor. `.sessional` is the scoped alternative, which closes the
        connection when its block ends.
  entry .EndpointSessional   the `Sessional` instance which lends an `Http2.Connection` to a scope on an `Http2.Endpoint`
    detail
        The class behind the `.sessional` given, named rather than anonymous so that its `Result`
        type — a capturing `Http2.Connection` — is stated exactly. `endpoint.session: connection
        ?=> …` connects the endpoint, completes the handshake, lends the live connection to the
        block, and tears it down (reader, writer and transport) when the block ends; the loan and
        the scope coincide, so no daemon holds the connection open afterwards. The block's result
        type is quantified outside it, so a value still borrowing the connection — a lazily
        streaming response body, an open `Http2.Stream` — cannot escape, while a memoized body
        can. Fetches within the block multiplex on the one connection and may run concurrently.
        It needs a `Monitor`, a `Probate`, a `Tactic[Async.Error]`, a `Tactic[Socket.Error]` and
        a `Socket.Event is Loggable`.
    entry #Self   `Http2.Endpoint[endpoint]`
    entry #Result   `Http2.Connection^`: the live connection the block receives
    entry #session   connects, lends the connection to the block, and closes it after
  entry .sessional   the given which makes `endpoint.session: connection ?=> …` work on an `Http2.Endpoint`
      An `EndpointSessional` for any `Http2.Endpoint` whose address is `Connectable` and
      `Showable`. It is in `Http2`'s companion scope, so no import is needed, but it is summoned
      only where a `Monitor`, a `Probate`, a `Tactic[Async.Error]`, a `Tactic[Socket.Error]` and
      a `Socket.Event is Loggable` are in scope: inside a `supervise` block with, for instance,
      `strategies.throwUnsafely` and `logging.silentLogging`.
  entry .Client   the HTTP/2 `Http.Client` given, which fetches from an `Http2.Endpoint` over h2c
    detail
        Holds `.http2`, the `Http.Client onto Http2.Endpoint[endpoint]` instance: an
        `Http.Request` sent to an `Http2.Endpoint` is carried over a prior-knowledge h2c
        connection, with the endpoint's `authority` as its `:authority` and `http` as its
        scheme.
    entry .http2   an `Http.Client` speaking HTTP/2 to an `Http2.Endpoint`; one connection per request, closed with the scope
        The given captures the `Monitor` and `Probate` of the context it is summoned in — the
        connection's daemons and its scope-tied teardown need them — so it resolves only inside
        a `supervise` block. Each request opens a fresh connection with `Endpoint#connect`,
        which lives until the scope ends; there is no pooling, so a program making many
        requests to one endpoint should use `endpoint.session` instead. It also needs a
        `Tactic[Http2.Error]`, a `Tactic[Async.Error]`, a `Tactic[Socket.Error]` and a
        `Tactic[Truncation.Error]`, and the request logs through the `Http.Event is Loggable` in
        scope.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import threads.virtualThreads
        import probates.cancelProbate
        import logging.silentLogging
        import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets

        supervise:
          import Http2.Client.http2

          val request =
            Http.Request(Http.Get, 2.0, t"localhost".as[Host], t"/", Nil, () => Http.emptyBody())

          val client = summon[Http.Client onto Http2.Endpoint[Endpoint[Tcp.Port]]]
          client.request(request, Http2.Endpoint(Endpoint(t"localhost", Port[Tcp](8080)), t"localhost"))
        ```
  entry .Frame   an HTTP/2 frame: a nine-byte header and a type-specific payload, decoded from and serialised to bytes
    detail
        The wire unit of HTTP/2 (RFC 7540 §4): a 24-bit length, an 8-bit type, 8 flag bits, a
        31-bit stream id, and a payload whose shape the type decides. `Frame` is an enum of the
        types the engine models, each case holding its decoded fields rather than raw flags:

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Frame
        row  `Headers(streamId, headerBlock, endStream, endHeaders)`  an HPACK block, with padding and any `PRIORITY` prefix already stripped
        row  `Continuation(streamId, headerBlock, endHeaders)`  the rest of a block too large for one `HEADERS` frame
        row  `Data(streamId, payload, endStream)`  body bytes, padding stripped
        row  `RstStream(streamId, errorCode)`  the peer abandoning a stream
        row  `Settings(settings, ack)`  a list of `Setting`s, or an empty acknowledgement
        row  `Ping(opaque, ack)`  eight opaque bytes, echoed back with `ack`
        row  `GoAway(lastStreamId, errorCode, debug)`  the peer closing the connection
        row  `WindowUpdate(streamId, increment)`  flow-control credit, for the connection on stream `0`
        row  `Ignored(typeId, streamId)`  an inbound frame of a type the engine does not model (`PRIORITY`, `PUSH_PROMISE`, an extension), decoded only to be discarded, as the protocol requires; it can never be serialised
        ```

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        val ack: Data = Http2.Frame.Settings(Nil, ack = true).serialize   // 00 00 00 04 01 00 00 00 00
        val (frame, end) = Http2.Frame.decode(ack, 0)
        frame == Http2.Frame.Settings(Nil, ack = true)
        ```

        A program speaking the protocol by hand sends `frame.serialize` on a `Duplex` and reads
        with a `FrameReader`, which reassembles frames from a socket's arbitrary chunks.
    refer telekinesis.FrameReader
    refer telekinesis.Http2.FrameType
    refer telekinesis.Http2.Flags
    entry .decode   decodes one frame from bytes at an offset, returning it and the offset just past it
        `Frame.decode(data, offset)` reads the nine-byte header at `offset` and the payload it
        declares, which must already be wholly present: a header or payload running past the
        end of `data` raises `Http2.Error` with reason `Truncated`, and bad padding or a
        `SETTINGS` payload which is not a multiple of six bytes raises one with a `Protocol`
        reason. A type id the engine does not know becomes `Frame.Ignored`. The result is the
        frame and the offset of the next one, so a buffer of several frames is decoded by
        iterating.
    entry #stream   the stream the frame belongs to; `0` for the connection-level `SETTINGS`, `PING` and `GOAWAY`
    entry #serialize   the frame as bytes, header included
        The nine-byte header — the payload length, the type's id, the flags the fields imply
        (`END_STREAM`, `END_HEADERS`, `ACK`) and the stream id — followed by the payload.
        Serialising an `Ignored` frame is a panic: it exists only to be discarded.
  entry .FrameType   the frame types the engine models, each with its wire id
    detail
        An enum of the eight types the engine decodes and sends: `Data` (`0x0`), `Headers`
        (`0x1`), `RstStream` (`0x3`), `Settings` (`0x4`), `Ping` (`0x6`), `GoAway` (`0x7`),
        `WindowUpdate` (`0x8`) and `Continuation` (`0x9`). `PRIORITY` (`0x2`) and `PUSH_PROMISE`
        (`0x5`) have no case: the client disables server push, and priority is skipped where it
        appears, so an inbound frame of either kind decodes as `Frame.Ignored`.
    entry .fromId   the frame type with a wire id, or `Unset` for one the engine does not model
    entry #id   the type's wire id, the byte after the length in a frame header
  entry .Flags   the flag bits of a frame header, and a test for one
    detail
        The bits of the flags byte, as the supported frame types use them: `EndStream` (`0x1`,
        on `DATA` and `HEADERS`), `Ack` (also `0x1`, on `SETTINGS` and `PING`: the same bit
        with a different meaning), `EndHeaders` (`0x4`, on `HEADERS` and `CONTINUATION`),
        `Padded` (`0x8`, on `DATA` and `HEADERS`) and `Priority` (`0x20`, on `HEADERS`).
        `Frame.decode` reads them into the frames' Boolean fields; they are exposed for a
        program examining raw headers.
    entry .EndStream   `0x1`: the frame ends its stream's sending half (`DATA`, `HEADERS`)
    entry .Ack   `0x1`: the frame acknowledges one of the same type (`SETTINGS`, `PING`)
    entry .EndHeaders   `0x4`: the header block is complete (`HEADERS`, `CONTINUATION`)
    entry .Padded   `0x8`: a pad length byte and trailing padding are present (`DATA`, `HEADERS`)
    entry .Priority   `0x20`: a five-byte priority prefix precedes the header block (`HEADERS`)
    entry .set   whether a flag bit is set in a flags byte: `Flags.set(flags, Flags.EndStream)`
  entry .ErrorCode   the HTTP/2 error codes carried by `RST_STREAM` and `GOAWAY`, with their numeric codes
    detail
        An enum of the fourteen codes of RFC 7540 §7, each with its `#code`: `NoError` (`0x0`),
        `ProtocolError` (`0x1`), `InternalError` (`0x2`), `FlowControlError` (`0x3`),
        `SettingsTimeout` (`0x4`), `StreamClosed` (`0x5`), `FrameSizeError` (`0x6`),
        `RefusedStream` (`0x7`), `Cancel` (`0x8`), `CompressionError` (`0x9`), `ConnectError`
        (`0xa`), `EnhanceYourCalm` (`0xb`), `InadequateSecurity` (`0xc`) and `Http11Required`
        (`0xd`). A `Frame.RstStream` or `Frame.GoAway` carries the raw `Long`, since a peer may
        send a code outside the list; `ErrorCode.NoError.code` is what a clean `#close` sends.
    entry #code   the code's numeric value, as a `Long`
  entry .SettingId   the identifiers of the `SETTINGS` parameters, with their numeric ids
    detail
        An enum of the six parameters of RFC 7540 §6.5.2, each with its `#id`:
        `HeaderTableSize` (`0x1`), `EnablePush` (`0x2`), `MaxConcurrentStreams` (`0x3`),
        `InitialWindowSize` (`0x4`), `MaxFrameSize` (`0x5`) and `MaxHeaderListSize` (`0x6`). A
        `Setting` holds the raw id rather than a `SettingId`, since an unknown identifier must be
        ignored rather than rejected. The engine acts on `InitialWindowSize` and `MaxFrameSize`
        from a peer, and sends `EnablePush` (as `0`, from the client) and `InitialWindowSize`.
    entry #id   the parameter's 16-bit identifier
  entry .Setting   one `SETTINGS` parameter: a 16-bit identifier and a 32-bit value
    detail
        `Setting(SettingId.InitialWindowSize.id, 65535)`. The id is raw so that a parameter the
        engine does not know is carried and ignored, as the protocol requires.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val settings = List(Http2.Setting(Http2.SettingId.InitialWindowSize.id, 65535))
        Http2.Frame.Settings(settings, ack = false).serialize
        ```
    entry #id   the parameter's identifier; `SettingId#id` for a known one
    entry #value   the parameter's value, as an unsigned 32-bit number in a `Long`
  entry .Error   raised for a malformed or protocol-violating HTTP/2 exchange, with a `Reason`
    detail
        _the HTTP/2 operation failed because `reason`_. Raised by `Frame.decode`, `FrameReader`,
        `Hpack#decode`, `Huffman.decode` and `PseudoHeaders` through a `Tactic[Http2.Error]`,
        and by `Connection#fetch` for the exchange it drives. Inside a connection's reader
        daemon the error tears that one connection down rather than escaping; an `https`
        session reports it as a `Connect.Error`.
    entry .Reason   why an HTTP/2 operation failed
      detail
          Each case renders as its message:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `Truncated`  _the HTTP/2 data ended unexpectedly_  a frame, header block or integer cut short
          row  `BadPreface`  _the connection preface was not valid_
          row  `BadHuffman`  _the Huffman-coded string was not valid_  a code longer than 30 bits, or padding which is not all ones
          row  `BadInteger`  _an HPACK integer was malformed or too large_
          row  `BadIndex(index)`  _the HPACK table index `index` was out of range_
          row  `BadFrameType(id)`  _the frame type `id` was not recognized_
          row  `FrameTooLarge`  _a frame exceeded the maximum permitted size_
          row  `FlowControl`  _a flow-control window was exceeded_
          row  `Protocol(message)`  _a protocol error occurred: `message`_  a bad connection preface, bad padding, a bad `SETTINGS` length, or a header block missing `:status`, `:method`, `:path` or `:authority`
          row  `GoAway(code)`  _the peer closed the connection with error code `code`_
          ```
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
    entry #reason   the reason, an `Http2.Error.Reason`
  entry .Event   what a connection logs: a request sent, and a `GOAWAY` received
    detail
        Every `Event` is tagged `Log.Network` and `Log.Protocol`. `Connection#fetch` logs
        `RequestSent(authority)` at `fine` — _sending an HTTP/2 request to `authority`_ — and a
        connection's reader logs `GoAway(lastStream)` as a warning — _received GOAWAY; the last
        processed stream was `lastStream`_ — before it stops. An `Http2.Event is Loggable` must
        be in scope for a fetch; `logging.silentLogging` discards them.
    entry .communicable   renders each event as a message

entry .Hpack   an HPACK codec: compresses and decompresses HTTP/2 header blocks with a dynamic table of its own
  detail
      HPACK (RFC 7541) is how HTTP/2 carries headers: each field is an index into a table of
      recently-seen fields, or a literal name and value, each Huffman-coded where that is
      shorter. An `Hpack` instance owns one direction's dynamic table, which changes with every
      block it encodes or decodes, so a connection keeps one codec for decoding inbound blocks
      and one for encoding outbound ones, and the two never mix.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val codec: Hpack^ = Hpack()

      val block: Data =
        codec.encode(List(Hpack.Entry(t":method", t"GET"), Hpack.Entry(t":path", t"/")))

      Hpack().decode(block).map(_.name)   // List(t":method", t":path")
      ```

      The codec is a stateful, exclusive capability: `Hpack()` returns `Hpack^`, and `#encode`
      and `#decode` are update methods. Hold it in a local or pass it as a parameter typed
      `Hpack^`, and do not call it from a closure or a local `def` which captures it — a
      captured reference is read-only, and the call is rejected — which is also why a codec
      belongs to exactly one reader or writer daemon.

      Fed successive blocks, a decoder's dynamic table grows, and each block can refer back to
      what the last one added (the example of RFC 7541 Appendix C.3):

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The first request: four fields, the authority sent as a literal
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            def hex(text: Text): Data = Array.from(text.s.grouped(2).map(Integer.parseInt(_, 16).toByte))

            val decoder: Hpack^ = Hpack()
            val first = decoder.decode(hex(t"828684410f7777772e6578616d706c652e636f6d"))
            first.map(_.value)   // GET, http, /, www.example.com
      frame
        label The second: the authority is now index 62, one byte, and a new field is added
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            def hex(text: Text): Data = Array.from(text.s.grouped(2).map(Integer.parseInt(_, 16).toByte))

            val decoder: Hpack^ = Hpack()
            val first = decoder.decode(hex(t"828684410f7777772e6578616d706c652e636f6d"))
            val second = decoder.decode(hex(t"828684be58086e6f2d6361636865"))
            second.map(_.value)   // GET, http, /, www.example.com, no-cache
      frame
        label The third: a custom field, with the two earlier additions still indexed
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            def hex(text: Text): Data = Array.from(text.s.grouped(2).map(Integer.parseInt(_, 16).toByte))

            val decoder: Hpack^ = Hpack()
            val first = decoder.decode(hex(t"828684410f7777772e6578616d706c652e636f6d"))
            val second = decoder.decode(hex(t"828684be58086e6f2d6361636865"))
            val third = decoder.decode(hex(t"828785bf400a637573746f6d2d6b65790c637573746f6d2d76616c7565"))
            third.map(_.value)   // GET, https, /index.html, www.example.com, custom-value
      ##
      ```

      The encoder is deliberately simple: every field goes out as a literal with incremental
      indexing, with its name and value each Huffman-coded or raw, whichever is shorter. That
      is correct and interoperable with any decoder, and keeps the two tables in step, but does
      not yet use the static table's name matches, so a block is larger than a tuned encoder
      would make it. Pseudo-headers must be ordered ahead of regular fields by the caller, as
      `PseudoHeaders` arranges.
  refer telekinesis.Hpack.Entry
  refer telekinesis.Hpack.Table
  refer telekinesis.Huffman
  refer telekinesis.Http2
  entry .apply   a fresh codec with an empty dynamic table, as `Hpack()` or `Hpack(maxTableSize)`
      The table's byte limit is 4096 by default, the protocol's initial `SETTINGS_HEADER_TABLE_SIZE`.
      A peer's dynamic table size update inside a block resizes it. The result is `Hpack^`: a
      fresh, exclusive capability.
  entry .Entry   one header field: a name and a value
    detail
        `Hpack.Entry(t":status", t"200")`. Names are lowercase on the wire, and pseudo-headers
        begin with `:`; `#size` is the field's cost in the dynamic table.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Hpack.Entry(t"content-type", t"application/grpc").size   // 12 + 16 + 32
        ```
    entry #name   the field's name, lowercase, such as `:path` or `content-type`
    entry #value   the field's value
    entry #size   the field's size for dynamic-table accounting: name length + value length + 32
  entry .Table   the HPACK address space: the 61-entry static table and a bounded, most-recent-first dynamic table
    detail
        An index from 1 to 61 names a field of the static table of RFC 7541 Appendix A, held in
        `.static`; 62 upwards names the dynamic table, most recently inserted first. The dynamic
        table is a FIFO bounded by a byte size (`#capacity`), evicting its oldest entries to fit.
        A `Table` is owned by its `Hpack` codec, which is itself confined to one daemon; a
        program needs it only to resolve or inspect indices by hand.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val table: Hpack.Table^ = new Hpack.Table()
        table.lookup(2)    // Hpack.Entry(t":method", t"GET")
        table.add(Hpack.Entry(t"x-trace", t"abc"))
        table.lookup(62)   // the entry just added
        ```
    entry .static   the 61 fields of the static table, index 1 at element 0
        `:authority`, `:method` `GET` and `POST`, `:path` `/` and `/index.html`, `:scheme`
        `http` and `https`, the seven common `:status` values, and the standard header names
        with empty values, in the RFC's order.
    entry #size   the dynamic table's current byte size, the sum of its entries' sizes
    entry #capacity   the dynamic table's byte limit
    entry #resize   sets the byte limit, evicting the oldest entries to fit: a dynamic table size update
    entry #add   inserts a field at the front of the dynamic table, evicting the oldest to stay within the limit
        A field larger than the whole table empties it and is not itself stored, as the RFC
        specifies.
    entry #lookup   the field at a 1-based index: static for 1 to 61, then dynamic; `Unset` if out of range
  entry #decode   decodes a header block into its fields, updating the dynamic table as the block directs
      Reads a complete block — the payload of a `HEADERS` frame, or of one and its
      `CONTINUATION`s joined — as a `List[Hpack.Entry]` in order. Indexed fields are resolved
      through the table, literals with incremental indexing are added to it, literals without
      indexing and never-indexed literals are not, and a dynamic table size update resizes it.
      Needs a `Tactic[Http2.Error]`: `BadIndex` for an index the table does not have,
      `BadInteger` for an integer of more than 28 bits, `BadHuffman` for an invalid Huffman
      string and `Truncated` for a block cut short. An update method: the codec must be held
      exclusively.
  entry #encode   encodes fields as a header block, each a literal with incremental indexing
      The inverse of `#decode`: every field is written as a literal with incremental indexing,
      its name and value each Huffman-coded where that is shorter and raw otherwise, and added to
      the dynamic table so that the decoder's table stays in step. Fields are written in the
      order given, so pseudo-headers must come first. An update method: the codec must be held
      exclusively.

entry .Huffman   the HPACK Huffman code: compresses and decompresses the strings of a header block
  detail
      The canonical code of RFC 7541 Appendix B, over the 256 byte values and an end-of-string
      symbol. `Hpack` uses it for every name and value where it is shorter than the raw bytes;
      a program needs it directly only to speak HPACK by hand.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import codepages.asciiCodepage

      val coded: Data = Huffman.encode(t"www.example.com".in[Data])   // 12 bytes, from 15
      Huffman.decode(coded).utf8                                        // t"www.example.com"
      ```
  refer telekinesis.Hpack
  entry .encode   Huffman-codes bytes, padding the final byte with one-bits
      Most-significant bit first, with the last partial byte padded with the leading bits of the
      end-of-string symbol, as the RFC requires. Never fails.
  entry .decode   decodes a Huffman-coded string back to its bytes
      Walks the bits against the code table, accepting and discarding the trailing padding.
      Needs a `Tactic[Http2.Error]`, raised with reason `BadHuffman` for a run of more than 30
      bits matching no symbol, or for padding which is eight bits or more or not all ones. The
      decoder is a linear scan of the table per bit, so it is correct rather than fast.

entry .FrameReader   reassembles whole HTTP/2 frames from a byte stream which arrives in arbitrary chunks
  detail
      A socket delivers bytes in whatever chunks the network makes, and a frame may span several
      or share one. `FrameReader(source)` takes ownership of a `Stream[Data] over Credit` — a
      `Duplex`'s `source` — buffers what it has read, and refills until a frame's nine-byte
      header and its declared payload are both present, so `#next` returns one decoded `Frame`
      at a time and `Unset` at a clean end of stream. It is the connection's single reader: an
      exclusive, stateful capability which consumes the endpoint it is given, like a parser.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def frames(duplex: Duplex): List[Http2.Frame] =
        val reader: FrameReader^ = FrameReader(duplex.source)
        var frames: List[Http2.Frame] = Nil
        var continue = true

        while continue do reader.next() match
          case frame: Http2.Frame => frames = frame :: frames
          case _                  => continue = false

        frames.reverse
      ```

      A server reads the client's 24-byte connection preface with `#expectPreface` before its
      first `#next`. A `Buffering` must be in scope, which sizes the reads.
  refer telekinesis.Http2.Frame
  refer telekinesis.Http2.ServerConnection
  entry .apply   a reader over a pull endpoint, which it consumes: `FrameReader(duplex.source)`
      Takes the endpoint by `consume`, so the caller gives up its own use of it: the reader is
      now its only reader. Needs a `Buffering` in scope. The result is `FrameReader^`, a fresh
      exclusive capability.
  entry #expectPreface   consumes and checks a connection preface ahead of the first frame
      `reader.expectPreface(preface)` reads exactly `preface.length` bytes and compares them: a
      mismatch, or the stream ending within them, raises `Http2.Error` with the `Protocol`
      reason _bad connection preface_. The server role's first read on a new connection, with
      the client preface `PRI * HTTP/2.0\r\n\r\nSM\r\n\r\n`. An update method.
  entry #next   the next whole frame, or `Unset` at a clean end of stream
      Blocks, refilling the endpoint, until a frame header and its declared payload are
      buffered, then decodes them with `Http2.Frame.decode`. A stream ending after a header but
      before its payload is complete raises `Http2.Error` with reason `Truncated`; a stream
      ending between frames is `Unset`. Needs a `Tactic[Http2.Error]`. An update method.

entry .PseudoHeaders   translates between `Http.Request`/`Http.Response` and HTTP/2 header blocks, in both directions
  detail
      HTTP/2 carries the request line and status line as pseudo-headers — `:method`, `:scheme`,
      `:authority` and `:path` on a request, `:status` on a response — ahead of the regular
      fields, which are lowercase. `PseudoHeaders` is the bridge between that and telekinesis's
      transport-agnostic `Http.Request` and `Http.Response`: `.request` and `.response` are the
      client's two directions, and `.requestOf` and `.entries` are the server's, each the
      inverse of the other.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val request =
        Http.Request(Http.Get, 2.0, t"example.com".as[Host], t"/index.html", Nil, () => Http.emptyBody())

      PseudoHeaders.request(request, t"https", t"example.com").map(_.name)
      // List(t":method", t":scheme", t":authority", t":path")
      ```
  refer telekinesis.Hpack.Entry
  refer telekinesis.Http2.Connection
  refer telekinesis.Http2.ServerConnection
  entry .request   the header block of a request: its four pseudo-headers, then its headers lowercased
      `PseudoHeaders.request(request, scheme, authority)`. An `Http.Request` carries neither its
      scheme nor the authority it is sent to, so both are given: `:method` from the request's
      method, `:scheme` as given, `:authority` as given (a browser omits a default port, as
      `localhost` rather than `localhost:443`), `:path` from the request's target, then each
      header with its name lowercased. `Http2.Connection#fetch` calls it.
  entry .response   an `Http.Response` from a decoded response block and a body stream
      `PseudoHeaders.response(headerBlock, body)`: `:status` selects the `Http.Status`, other
      pseudo-headers are dropped, and the remaining fields become the response's headers; the
      body is a flowing `Http.Body` over the given chain of byte blocks. Raises `Http2.Error`
      with a `Protocol` reason, _missing :status_, when the block has no `:status` or one which
      is not a known status code.
  entry .requestOf   an `Http.Request` from a decoded request block and a body spring
      `PseudoHeaders.requestOf(headerBlock, body)`: `:method` and `:path` become the method and
      target, `:authority` becomes the host (any port stripped, as for an HTTP/1.1 `Host`
      header), other pseudo-headers are dropped, and the remaining fields become the request's
      headers; the version is 2.0. The body is a `Spring[Data]` the request takes ownership of,
      typically one over the stream's inbound `DATA` frames. Needs a `Tactic[Http2.Error]`,
      raised with a `Protocol` reason for a block missing `:authority`, `:path` or `:method`, or
      whose `:authority` is not a host. The server role's inverse of `.request`.
  entry .entries   the header block of a response: `:status` first, then its headers lowercased, minus connection-specific ones
      `PseudoHeaders.entries(response)`: `:status` as the status code's digits, then each header
      lowercased, with `connection`, `keep-alive`, `transfer-encoding`, `upgrade` and
      `proxy-connection` removed, as HTTP/2 forbids them. The server role's inverse of
      `.response`; the block `ServerConnection#sendHeaders` sends.

entry .FlowWindow   one HTTP/2 flow-control send window: a byte budget the peer grants, acquired in chunks and refilled by `WINDOW_UPDATE`
  detail
      The send-side credit of a connection or a stream (RFC 7540 §6.9), as a counter a sending
      fiber draws from and an inbound `WINDOW_UPDATE` tops up. `#acquire` blocks until at least
      one byte is available and takes up to the amount asked, so a large body drains as the
      window opens; `#release` adds credit and wakes every waiter. The waiting is on a lock
      condition rather than a monitor, so a blocked virtual thread releases its carrier.
      `Http2.Connection` and `Http2.ServerConnection` keep one per connection and one per
      stream; a program needs the class only to model flow control itself.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import threads.virtualThreads
      import probates.cancelProbate

      supervise:
        val window = FlowWindow(10)
        val first = window.acquire(4)     // 4
        val second = window.acquire(100)  // 6: all that remains
        val third: Promise[Int] = Promise()
        async(third.offer(window.acquire(5)))   // parks: the window is empty
        window.release(3)
        third.await()                     // 3
      ```

      The class is declared in the `telekinesis` package but is not exported to the `soundness`
      umbrella, so it is reached as `telekinesis.FlowWindow`.
  refer telekinesis.Http2.Connection
  entry #acquire   takes up to `n` bytes of credit, blocking while the window is empty
      Returns at most `n`, and at least one byte when `n` is positive: whatever the window holds
      up to `n`, so a caller sending a body loops until it has sent everything. Blocks the calling fiber while the
      window is at or below zero, until `#release` wakes it.
  entry #release   adds credit to the window and wakes every fiber waiting on it
      The effect of an inbound `WINDOW_UPDATE`. All waiters are woken, each then taking what it
      can, so two senders parked on an empty window both proceed after one release.

entry .UrlSessional   the `Sessional` instance which opens one connection to an HTTP or HTTPS URL's origin and lends it to a scope
  detail
      The class behind the `httpUrlSessional` given, named rather than anonymous so that its
      `Result` type — an `Http.Session` which captures the connection — is stated exactly.
      `url.session: session ?=> …` connects to the URL's host and port once, lends the
      connection to the block as an `Http.Session` on which `session.fetch(request)` makes an
      exchange, and closes it when the block ends. The scheme decides the session's form, and
      the form is fixed for its lifetime:

      - `http` is a sequential HTTP/1.1 session over one kept-alive connection: one exchange
        at a time, each response's body ending exactly where the next response begins. Leaving
        a response unconsumed does not derail it — the next fetch drains what remains of the
        previous body to reach the boundary — but a `101 Switching Protocols` response is
        refused, since its body would be the upgraded protocol's unending stream.
      - `https` connects with TLS offering `h2` then `http/1.1` by ALPN. If the server
        selects `h2`, the session is multiplexed over an `Http2.Connection` behind the same
        sequential contract, each fetch's body drained within the fetch so the response is a
        plain value; otherwise it is the sequential form. HTTP/2 and asynchronous failures are
        reported as `Connect.Error`.

      The block's result is quantified outside it, so a value still borrowing the connection —
      a response body streaming from it — cannot escape; a memoized one can. The instance needs
      an `Online`, a `Socket.Backend`, the TCP `Socket.Option`s, a `Buffering`, a `Tls` and a
      `Tactic[Connect.Error]`, raised with reason `Unknown` for a scheme other than `http` or
      `https`, `Dns` for a URL with no host, and the socket's reason — `Dns`, `Refused`,
      `Timeout`, `Ssl(Handshake)` — when the connection cannot be made.
  refer telekinesis.httpUrlSessional
  refer telekinesis.Http2.Connection
  entry #Self   the URL type, a subtype of `HttpUrl`
  entry #Result   `Http.Session^`: the session the block receives
  entry #session   connects to the URL's origin, lends the session to the block, and closes the connection after

entry .httpUrlSessional   the given which makes `url.session: session ?=> …` work on an HTTP or HTTPS URL
    A `UrlSessional` for any `url <: HttpUrl`, bounded so that a `url"https://…"` literal, whose
    type is a scheme-refined subtype, resolves it. It is a top-level given, so it is imported by
    name — `import soundness.httpUrlSessional` — and it needs an `Online`, a `Socket.Backend`
    (such as `socketBackends.javaBaseSockets`), the TCP `Socket.Option`s, a `Buffering`, a `Tls`
    and a `Tactic[Connect.Error]` in scope.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.httpUrlSessional
    import socketBackends.javaBaseSockets
    import internetAccess.online
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import logging.silentLogging

    val request =
      Http.Request(Http.Get, 1.1, t"example.com".as[Host], t"/", Nil, () => Http.emptyBody())

    url"https://example.com".session: session ?=>
      session.fetch(request).status
      session.fetch(request).status
    ```

    Over `https`, the session speaks HTTP/2 if the server's ALPN selects it and HTTP/1.1
    otherwise; over `http` it is always HTTP/1.1 with keep-alive. `UrlSessional` describes the
    two forms and the errors.
